QB.add(48,[{"id":48001,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"Two tumours are compared. In tumour X, a growth-control gene contains a deletion. In tumour Y, the same gene has a normal sequence but is switched off by chemical tagging of its promoter.<br><br>Which statement best describes the two changes?","o":["X is a genetic change; Y is an epigenetic change of gene expression","Both are genetic changes, because both of them alter the DNA code of the gene","X is epigenetic; Y is a genetic change in the DNA sequence","Both are epigenetic, because both affect gene expression","Neither can cause cancer, because only translocations can"],"e":"Cancer is a genetic disease caused by changes to the DNA (mutations, deletions, translocations) plus epigenetic changes such as promoter methylation. Epigenetic changes go beyond the genome: they change gene expression, not the DNA code. So the deletion is genetic and the promoter methylation is epigenetic; both can produce abnormal neoplastic growth.","w":["Promoter methylation does not alter the DNA code.","This reverses the two types of change.","A deletion changes the DNA sequence, so it is genetic.","Mutations, deletions and epigenetic changes can all cause cancer."],"k":"Genetic = DNA code changes; epigenetic = expression changes.","rn":[["","What are epigenetic changes?","What are epigenetic changes?","Altered gene expression; Genome/chromatin modifications; Not DNA sequence changes"],["","What is an example of an epigenetic change in cancer?","What is an example of an epigenetic change in cancer?","Promoter methylation"]]},{"id":48002,"t":"Molecular Basis of Cancer","s":"Why is understanding the mutated proteins produced in cancer considered so important?","o":["It allows new treatments to target the specific molecules that are altered","It shows which cancers can be diagnosed by looking at tissue alone","It proves that surgery is never needed once the mutation in a tumour is known","It shows that chemotherapy is fully targeted to cancer cells","It identifies which patients will never develop a recurrence"],"e":"Molecular biology studies the composition, structure and interactions of cellular molecules, i.e. nucleic acids and proteins. Understanding the mutations and mutated proteins in cancer is really important for developing new treatments. Many treatments now target specific molecules that are overexpressed or mutated in cancers, unlike older chemotherapy and radiotherapy, which are not so targeted.","w":["Diagnosis now also uses molecular biology, not tissue alone.","Surgery is still a key treatment and in many cases the cure.","Chemotherapy is described as not so targeted.","Cancer can come back if not all the cells are removed."],"k":"Knowing the mutated molecules enables targeted therapy.","rn":[["","What do targeted treatments target?","What do targeted treatments target?","Overexpressed or mutated molecules"]]},{"id":48003,"t":"Targeting the Hallmarks","s":"Why does the lecturer avoid always calling surgery a \"cure\" for cancer?","o":["The cancer can come back if not all the cells were removed","Surgery is no longer used as a treatment for most cancers","Surgery always spreads cancer cells into the bloodstream and lymph nodes","Surgery is less effective than any form of chemotherapy","Surgery can only be used for benign tumours, not cancers"],"e":"Surgery is still a key treatment and in a lot of cases can be the cure. However, the lecturer avoids always saying \"cure\" because the cancer can come back if not all the cells were removed. Chemotherapy and radiotherapy can be quite toxic and are not so targeted.","w":["Surgery is still described as a key treatment.","This is not stated in the lecture.","No such comparison is made.","Surgery is a key treatment for cancers."],"k":"Surgery can cure, but remaining cells can cause recurrence.","rn":[["","How do cancer cells avoid the immune system?","How do cancer cells avoid the immune system?","Change expressed surface molecules"],["","Why are natural cell processes a cause of cancer?","Why are natural cell processes a cause of cancer?","We constantly mutate"],["","Which cancer cells secrete VEGFA?","Which cancer cells secrete VEGFA?","Hypoxic cancer cells"]]},{"id":48004,"t":"Molecular Basis of Cancer","s":"Put the multistep model of carcinogenesis in the correct order.","o":["Genome-stability mutation → mutator phenotype → cancer-gene mutations → invasion and metastasis","Cancer-gene mutations → genome-stability mutation → mutator phenotype → invasion and metastasis","Mutator phenotype → genome-stability mutation → invasion → cancer-gene mutations","Invasion → genome-stability mutation → cancer-gene mutations → mutator phenotype","Genome-stability mutation → invasion → mutator phenotype → cancer-gene mutations"],"e":"X-rays, chemicals and endogenous processes cause an early mutation in a gene responsible for genome stability (DNA repair, mismatch repair, DNA replication or chromosomal segregation). This produces a mutator phenotype with multiple mutations. Mutations then hit cancer-associated genes (ras, Rb, p53, p16), leading to cancer progression, invasion and metastasis.","w":["The genome-stability mutation comes first.","The mutator phenotype follows the genome-stability mutation.","Invasion is the final stage.","Invasion comes after the cancer-gene mutations."],"k":"Stability gene → mutator phenotype → oncogenes/TSGs → spread.","rn":[["","Why does apoptosis fail in cancer cells with many mutations?","Why does apoptosis fail in cancer cells with many mutations?","Lack of genome stability"]]},{"id":48005,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"What term describes the state in which cells mutate a lot because genome stability has been lost?","a":["mutator phenotype","the mutator phenotype"],"e":"In the multistep model, an early mutation hits a gene that keeps the genome stable, such as BRCA1 or BRCA2. DNA repair is overwhelmed and a mutator phenotype develops, so mutations build up. These eventually hit cancer-associated genes such as ras, Rb, p53 and p16.","k":"Mutator phenotype = high mutation rate after loss of genome stability.","rn":[["","Epigenetic changes alter phenotype or genotype?","Epigenetic changes alter phenotype or genotype?","Phenotype only"]]},{"id":48006,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"In the multistep model, the early mutation is in a gene responsible for genome stability. Which process is NOT one of the genome-stability functions listed?","o":["Angiogenesis","DNA repair","Mismatch repair","DNA replication","Chromosomal segregation"],"e":"The slide lists the early mutation as being in a gene responsible for genome stability: DNA repair, mismatch repair, DNA replication or chromosomal segregation. Angiogenesis is a hallmark of cancer (inducing new blood vessels), not a genome-stability function.","w":["DNA repair is a genome-stability function.","Mismatch repair is a genome-stability function.","DNA replication is a genome-stability function.","Chromosomal segregation is a genome-stability function."],"k":"Genome stability: DNA repair, mismatch repair, replication, segregation.","rn":[["","What is angiogenesis?","What is angiogenesis?","New blood vessels; From pre-existing ones"],["","What is the key growth factor in angiogenesis?","What is the key growth factor in angiogenesis?","VEGF"],["","Why must a cancer induce angiogenesis to invade?","Why must a cancer induce angiogenesis to invade?","Blood supply; Oxygen; Nutrients"]]},{"id":48007,"t":"Molecular Basis of Cancer","s":"A cell accumulates many mutations. Normally it would die by programmed cell death, but it survives. Which explanation from the multistep model fits best?","o":["Apoptosis fails, partly because the genome is unstable","Contact inhibition becomes stronger as mutations build up","DNA repair becomes more efficient with each new mutation","The cell's telomeres shorten faster than in normal cells","The immune system has removed all neighbouring cells"],"e":"Normally a cell with lots of mutations goes into apoptosis (programmed cell death). In carcinogenesis this does not happen, partly because of the unstable genome. p53 is the \"guardian of the genome\": if it is mutated, the cell cycle carries on despite mutations and damaged cells do not go into apoptosis.","w":["Transformed cells lose contact inhibition.","Repair is overwhelmed, not improved.","In cancer, telomere caps do not degrade normally.","Immune effects are not the explanation given."],"k":"Damaged cells survive because apoptosis fails.","rn":[["","Why does apoptosis fail in cancer cells with many mutations?","Why does apoptosis fail in cancer cells with many mutations?","Lack of genome stability"]]},{"id":48008,"t":"Molecular Basis of Cancer","s":"Why are cancers relatively uncommon in young people?","o":["DNA repair works really well when we are younger","Young people are never exposed to any mutagens such as X-rays or chemicals","Young cells do not copy their DNA at all","Oncogenes are only present after middle age","Children have no tumour suppressor genes"],"e":"Mutations happen all the time from X-rays, chemicals and endogenous processes (\"while we're sitting here we are mutating\"). DNA repair fixes most of them, and it works really well when we are younger, so there are not many cancers in young people (though there are some). As we get older, repair does not work as well.","w":["Mutagens are everywhere, including in everyday life.","Young cells copy their DNA when they divide.","Proto-oncogenes are normal genes present throughout life.","Tumour suppressor genes are normal genes present in everyone."],"k":"Repair declines with age, so cancer rises with age.","rn":[["","What happens to DNA repair as we age?","What happens to DNA repair as we age?","Works less well"],["","Why are cancers uncommon in young people?","Why are cancers uncommon in young people?","DNA repair works well"]]},{"id":48009,"t":"Molecular Basis of Cancer","s":"Which protein is known as the \"guardian of the genome\"?","a":["p53","tp53","tumour protein 53","tumor protein 53"],"e":"p53, coded by TP53, is known as the guardian of the genome. If it is mutated, the cell cycle carries on even with mutations, damaged cells do not go into apoptosis and the cancer starts to take hold. It is a tumour suppressor that gives the growth inhibitory signal at the restriction point in G1.","k":"p53 = guardian of the genome.","rn":[["","What is p53 known as?","What is p53 known as?","Guardian of the genome"]]},{"id":48010,"t":"Molecular Basis of Cancer","s":"A person carries a mutated BRCA1 gene. Which statement about their cancer risk is correct?","o":["Their likelihood of cancer is higher, but cancer is not certain","They will definitely develop breast cancer","They are protected from cancer, because BRCA1 is an oncogene that repairs DNA","Their risk is unchanged unless both parents carry the gene","They can only develop retinoblastoma"],"e":"BRCA1 and BRCA2 are genome-stability genes and tumour suppressors. Having the mutated genes does not mean someone will definitely get cancer, but they have a higher likelihood. This is why predictive genetic tests and, in some cases, preventive surgery are discussed.","w":["Cancer is not certain.","BRCA1 is a tumour suppressor, not an oncogene.","A mutated gene raises risk.","Retinoblastoma is linked to Rb, not BRCA1."],"k":"BRCA mutation = higher likelihood, not certainty.","rn":[["","Does carrying BRCA1/2 mutations mean definite cancer?","Does carrying BRCA1/2 mutations mean definite cancer?","No; Higher likelihood"]]},{"id":48011,"t":"Assays of Oncogenic Transformation","s":"In the hamster embryo transformation assay, which enzyme is used to separate the embryo cells into single cells?","a":["trypsin"],"e":"In the primary culture assay of oncogenic transformation, cells from a hamster embryo are enzymatically dissociated with trypsin into single cells. Trypsin is also used to subculture cells in cell culture. Single cells are seeded into dishes and treated with radiation or chemicals, with an untreated control.","k":"Trypsin dissociates embryo cells."},{"id":48012,"t":"Assays of Oncogenic Transformation","s":"Which sequence correctly describes the transformation assay?","o":["Dissociate embryo cells → seed single cells → treat with radiation/chemicals → stain and score colonies at 8–10 days","Treat the whole embryo with X-ray → dissociate the cells → inject them into a mouse → score tumours at 8–10 days","Seed whole embryo tissue → stain → treat with chemicals → count colonies after 24 hours","Dissociate cells → transfect DNA → seed → count colonies at 8–10 days","Stain embryo cells → dissociate with trypsin → treat with radiation → count after one month"],"e":"In the assay, a hamster embryo is enzymatically dissociated (trypsin), the cells are seeded as single cells into dishes, and they are treated with radiation or chemicals (e.g. X-ray), with an untreated control dish. Colonies are scored after 8–10 days after staining. Cells can be fixed with methanol and stained with Giemsa.","w":["Injection into mice belongs to the DNA transfection experiment.","Cells are seeded as single cells and treated before staining.","Transfection belongs to the oncogene detection experiment.","Staining comes last, at 8–10 days."],"k":"Assay: dissociate → seed → treat → stain/score at 8–10 days.","rn":[["","When are colonies scored in the transformation assay?","When are colonies scored in the transformation assay?","8-10 days; After staining"],["","Which stain is used to score the colonies?","Which stain is used to score the colonies?","Giemsa stain"]]},{"id":48013,"t":"Assays of Oncogenic Transformation","s":"Which stain can be used, after methanol fixation, to stain the colonies in the transformation assay?","a":["giemsa","giemsa stain"],"e":"Colonies in the transformation assay are scored after 8–10 days after staining. The speaker notes say cells can be fixed with methanol and stained with Giemsa stain. Transformed clones are identified morphologically, by their shape.","k":"Methanol fix + Giemsa stain.","rn":[["","What are the cells fixed with before Giemsa staining?","What are the cells fixed with before Giemsa staining?","Methanol"],["","Which stain is used to score the colonies?","Which stain is used to score the colonies?","Giemsa stain"]]},{"id":48014,"t":"Assays of Oncogenic Transformation","s":"After X-ray treatment, a culture dish shows a dense, darkly stained pile of cells in a random arrangement, while the control dish shows an even, orderly layer. What property have the treated cells lost?","o":["Contact inhibition","Apoptosis resistance","Tyrosine kinase activity","Telomerase expression","Oestrogen receptor expression"],"e":"Normal untransformed embryo cells show contact inhibition and are orderly. Radiation-transformed cells are densely stained, piled up and randomly arranged. X-ray radiation caused mutations, so the cells lost contact inhibition and kept growing on top of each other, which looks like a tumour in the body.","w":["Transformed cells resist cell death rather than losing that resistance.","Tyrosine kinase activity is linked to BCR-ABL and EGFR, not this assay.","Telomerase is linked to immortality, not to piling up in the dish.","Oestrogen receptors are not part of this assay."],"k":"Transformed cells lose contact inhibition and pile up.","rn":[["","Why do transformed cells lose contact inhibition?","Why do transformed cells lose contact inhibition?","Mutations from radiation"],["","What are the 2 features of normal untransformed embryo cells?","What are the 2 features of normal untransformed embryo cells?","Contact inhibition; Orderly"]]},{"id":48015,"t":"Assays of Oncogenic Transformation","s":"What term describes normal cells growing until they touch each other and then stopping?","a":["contact inhibition"],"e":"Contact inhibition is how normal body cells work: they grow until they touch each other, then stop. For example, in wound healing they stop once the wound is closed. Transformed cells lose contact inhibition and keep growing on top of each other.","k":"Contact inhibition = stop growing on contact.","rn":[["","What is contact inhibition?","What is contact inhibition?","Cells grow until touching; Then stop growing"]]},{"id":48016,"t":"Assays of Oncogenic Transformation","s":"Which everyday process is given as an example of contact inhibition in the body?","o":["Cells stopping division once a wound is closed","Blood vessels growing towards a hypoxic tumour","Hormones stimulating growth of breast tissue","Cells piling up after exposure to X-rays","Lymphocytes destroying a few cancer cells"],"e":"Normal cells grow until they touch each other, then stop. In wound healing, cells stop once the wound is closed. Transformed cells, by contrast, keep growing on top of each other.","w":["This is angiogenesis.","Hormonal stimulation is not the example given.","Piling up is the loss of contact inhibition.","This relates to avoiding immune destruction."],"k":"Wound closure shows contact inhibition."},{"id":48017,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"A 45-year-old woman has a long-term diet very high in red meat, saturated fat and salt and takes little exercise. In which factor category does this risk fall?","o":["Diet and lack of exercise","Reproductive life and hormones","Environmental pollutants","Natural cell processes","Inherited genes"],"e":"Factors that can cause cancer include smoking, diet and lack of exercise (too much red meat, saturated fat, salt), reproductive life, viruses, environmental pollutants, hormones, ionising radiation and sunlight, inherited genes, translocations, other acquired mutations and natural cell processes. Cheap processed foods are high in saturated fats, and eating healthily can be expensive.","w":["Reproductive life includes not having children and sexual promiscuity.","Pollutants include soot and traffic pollution.","Natural cell processes are mutations from normal cell activity.","Inherited genes are, for example, BRCA1 and BRCA2."],"k":"Red meat, saturated fat, salt, low exercise = diet factor.","rn":[["","Besides diet, what lifestyle factor raises cancer risk?","Besides diet, what lifestyle factor raises cancer risk?","Lack of exercise"]]},{"id":48018,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"Which cancer risk factor is given as a rare success where the causal agent can be removed, cutting diagnoses of cervical cancer?","o":["HPV (human papillomavirus), through vaccination","Smoking, through a ban on smoking in public places","Particulate matter, through clean air laws","Red meat, through dietary advice","Oestrogen, through hormone replacement therapy"],"e":"Viruses are one of the factors that can cause cancer, with HPV as the example. The HPV vaccine has really cut cervical cancer diagnoses, a rare success where the causal agent can be removed. Other factors include smoking, pollutants, diet and hormones.","w":["Smoking is a major risk but is not the example given here.","Clean air laws are not described.","Dietary advice is not the example given.","HRT is mentioned for people with too little oestrogen."],"k":"HPV vaccine cuts cervical cancer.","rn":[["","What does HPV stand for?","What does HPV stand for?","Human papillomavirus"],["","Which virus is the example of a cancer-causing virus?","Which virus is the example of a cancer-causing virus?","HPV (human papillomavirus)"]]},{"id":48019,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"A city worker breathes in traffic soot for decades and develops chronic lung inflammation. How does the lecture link this exposure to cancer?","o":["Particulates irritate the lungs; cytokines recruited to the inflammation can promote cancer","Particulates insert viral DNA into the lung cells, and this viral DNA then acts as an oncogene","Particulates raise oestrogen levels, which drives lung cell growth","Particulates cause a translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22","Particulates switch off DNA repair genes by promoter methylation in every case"],"e":"Environmental factors and pollutants include particulate matter such as soot and traffic pollution. Particulates irritate the lungs, causing inflammation, and the cytokines recruited to the inflammation can lead to cancer. This is the enabling characteristic of tumour-promoting inflammation.","w":["Viruses are a separate factor.","Hormones are a separate factor.","The 9;22 translocation is the Philadelphia chromosome in CML.","This mechanism is not described for particulates."],"k":"Pollutants → inflammation → cytokines → tumour-promoting inflammation.","rn":[["","How do pollutant particulates promote cancer?","How do pollutant particulates promote cancer?","Irritate lungs; Cause inflammation"]]},{"id":48020,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"Which pairing of risk factor with the example given on the slide is correct?","o":["Translocations: Philadelphia chromosome, BCR-ABL","Inherited genes: EGFR and HER2 overexpression","Viruses: BRCA1 and BRCA2","Hormones: particulate matter","Other acquired gene mutations: HPV"],"e":"The slide pairs translocations with the Philadelphia chromosome (BCR-ABL), inherited genes with BRCA1 and BRCA2, viruses with HPV, hormones with high oestrogen, tamoxifen and ER status, and other acquired gene mutations with overexpression of EGFR and HER2.","w":["EGFR overexpression is an acquired mutation.","BRCA1/2 are inherited genes.","Particulates are environmental pollutants.","HPV is a virus."],"k":"Match each risk factor with its slide example.","rn":[["","What is the translocation example of a cancer cause?","What is the translocation example of a cancer cause?","Philadelphia chromosome (BCR-ABL)"]]},{"id":48021,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"A patient's breast cancer shows high levels of oestrogen receptor. What does this predict about treatment?","o":["Tamoxifen is likely to work","Tamoxifen will not work","Surgery is no longer needed","HER2 inhibitors are the only option","Telomerase inhibitors are required"],"e":"If a patient's cancer has high levels of oestrogen receptor (ER), certain treatments such as tamoxifen will work. Tamoxifen will not work in patients without high ER levels, and other treatments work in those patients. ER comes from the US spelling \"estrogen\".","w":["Tamoxifen fails in patients without high ER.","Surgery remains a key treatment.","HER2 status is a separate target.","Telomerase inhibitors target immortality, not ER."],"k":"ER-positive → tamoxifen works.","rn":[["","Which drug is linked to oestrogen receptor status (ER+/-)?","Which drug is linked to oestrogen receptor status (ER+/-)?","Tamoxifen"]]},{"id":48022,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"Why is oestrogen described as \"a difficult one\" as a cancer risk factor?","o":["High oestrogen is a risk, but some people have too little and need HRT","Oestrogen protects against all cancers in every patient, whatever their age","Oestrogen has no link with any cancer type","Oestrogen only affects cancers in men","Oestrogen levels cannot be measured"],"e":"High oestrogen levels are a cancer risk. However, some people have too little oestrogen and go on hormone replacement therapy (HRT), so the balance is difficult. ER status also guides treatment, as tamoxifen works in cancers with high oestrogen receptor levels.","w":["High oestrogen is a risk.","High oestrogen is listed as a risk factor.","It is not restricted to men.","ER levels are measured to guide treatment."],"k":"Oestrogen: too much is a risk, too little may need HRT.","rn":[["","Which hormone factor raises cancer risk?","Which hormone factor raises cancer risk?","High oestrogen levels"]]},{"id":48023,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"The Philadelphia chromosome is a translocation between which two chromosomes?","o":["9 and 22","9 and 21","8 and 14","13 and 17","11 and 22"],"e":"The Philadelphia chromosome is observed in most patients with chronic myelogenous leukaemia (CML). It is a translocation between chromosome 9 and 22, which fuses BCR and ABL into a BCR-ABL gene with very high tyrosine kinase activity. The results are 9q+ and 22q− (the Philadelphia chromosome).","w":["Chromosome 21 is not involved.","These chromosomes are not involved.","These chromosomes are not involved.","Chromosome 11 is not involved."],"k":"Philadelphia = t(9;22).","rn":[["","Philadelphia chromosome is a translocation between which chromosomes?","Philadelphia chromosome is a translocation between which chromosomes?","9 and 22"],["","Which 2 genes fuse in the Philadelphia chromosome?","Which 2 genes fuse in the Philadelphia chromosome?","BCR; ABL"]]},{"id":48024,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"Which fusion gene is produced by the Philadelphia chromosome?","a":["bcr-abl","bcr abl","bcrabl","bcr-abl fusion","bcr-abl1"],"e":"Parts of chromosomes 9 and 22 swap over. BCR is on one chromosome and ABL on the other; the swap joins them into a BCR-ABL fusion gene. The fusion has very high tyrosine kinase activity, so cells divide and proliferate uncontrollably.","k":"Philadelphia chromosome → BCR-ABL.","rn":[["","Which 2 genes fuse in the Philadelphia chromosome?","Which 2 genes fuse in the Philadelphia chromosome?","BCR; ABL"],["","What activity is very high in the BCR-ABL fusion?","What activity is very high in the BCR-ABL fusion?","Tyrosine kinase activity"],["","What is the translocation example of a cancer cause?","What is the translocation example of a cancer cause?","Philadelphia chromosome (BCR-ABL)"]]},{"id":48025,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"A patient with chronic myelogenous leukaemia (CML) has a BCR-ABL fusion. Which sequence explains how it causes the leukaemia?","o":["Chromosome swap → fusion gene → very high tyrosine kinase activity → uncontrolled proliferation","Chromosome loss → no tyrosine kinase activity → failed apoptosis → leukaemia","Promoter methylation → silenced ABL gene → loss of contact inhibition in blood cells → leukaemia","Viral infection → truncated receptor → loss of ligand binding → leukaemia","Inherited BRCA mutation → mutator phenotype → fusion gene → leukaemia"],"e":"In the Philadelphia chromosome, parts of chromosomes 9 and 22 swap over, joining BCR and ABL into a BCR-ABL fusion gene. The fusion has a very high amount of tyrosine kinase activity, a phosphorylation activity that sends signals down to the nucleus. Cells divide and proliferate uncontrollably, giving this type of leukaemia.","w":["It is a swap (translocation), and the kinase activity is very high.","This describes an epigenetic change, not the Philadelphia chromosome.","This resembles the v-erbB truncated EGFR, not BCR-ABL.","BCR-ABL arises from a translocation, not a BRCA mutation."],"k":"BCR-ABL: high tyrosine kinase activity → proliferation.","rn":[["","What activity is very high in the BCR-ABL fusion?","What activity is very high in the BCR-ABL fusion?","Tyrosine kinase activity"]]},{"id":48026,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"After the 9;22 swap, which chromosome is the Philadelphia chromosome?","o":["22q− (the shortened chromosome 22)","9q+ (the lengthened chromosome 9)","22q+ (the lengthened chromosome 22)","9q− (the shortened chromosome 9)","A normal, unchanged chromosome 22"],"e":"The slide shows normal chromosomes 9 and 22 swapping pieces. The results are 9q+ (chromosome 9, now longer) and 22q− (chromosome 22, now shorter). The shortened 22q− is the Philadelphia chromosome (Ph1).","w":["9q+ is the other product of the swap.","Chromosome 22 becomes shorter, not longer.","Chromosome 9 becomes longer, not shorter.","The Philadelphia chromosome is the altered chromosome 22."],"k":"Ph1 = 22q−.","rn":[["","Image card: What is the abnormal chromosome on the right called? Which fusion gene and disease?","What is the abnormal chromosome on the right called? Which fusion gene and disease?","Philadelphia chromosome (22q-); BCR-ABL fusion; CML (chronic myelogenous leukaemia)"]]},{"id":48027,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"Why is a coloured (painted) karyotype useful when looking for a translocation such as the Philadelphia chromosome?","o":["It makes it easy to see when a piece of one chromosome has moved onto another","It measures the tyrosine kinase activity produced by each of the painted chromosomes","It shows which genes are methylated on each chromosome","It counts the number of mitoses in the sample","It shows whether a tumour is benign or malignant"],"e":"A coloured karyotype paints all the chromosomes in different colours and numbers them 1–22, X and Y. Painting makes it easy to see when a piece of one chromosome has moved onto another, which is a translocation. The Philadelphia chromosome is a translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22.","w":["Kinase activity is not shown by painting.","Methylation is epigenetic and is not shown this way.","Mitotic counts are part of tumour grading.","Benign versus malignant is a histological judgement."],"k":"Chromosome painting reveals translocations."},{"id":48028,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts oncogenes with tumour suppressor genes?","o":["Oncogenes need one mutated allele; tumour suppressors usually need both","Oncogenes need both alleles mutated; tumour suppressors need only one mutated allele","Both types act in a recessive manner","Oncogene mutations cause loss of function","Tumour suppressor mutations cause gain of function"],"e":"Oncogene mutations give a gain of function (more protein, or a more active product), act in a dominant manner and need only one mutated allele; more than 100 are known. Tumour suppressor mutations give a loss of function, are mainly recessive because both alleles must be mutated, although haploinsufficiency can occur; at least 15 are known.","w":["This reverses the rule.","Oncogenes act dominantly.","Oncogene mutations are gain of function.","Tumour suppressor mutations are loss of function."],"k":"Oncogene: dominant, one allele. TSG: recessive, two alleles.","rn":[["","Why are tumour suppressor genes mainly recessive?","Why are tumour suppressor genes mainly recessive?","Both alleles must be mutated"],["","Oncogenes vs tumour suppressor genes: effect of mutation","Oncogenes vs tumour suppressor genes: effect of mutation","Oncogene: gain of function; TSG: loss of function"],["","Oncogenes and tumour suppressor genes start as what?","Oncogenes and tumour suppressor genes start as what?","Normal genes; Needed for everyday processes"]]},{"id":48029,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"Why are many more oncogenes known (more than 100) than tumour suppressor genes (at least 15)?","o":["Only one allele needs mutating for an oncogene, which is far more likely than both alleles","Oncogenes are found only in viruses, which are very common","Tumour suppressor genes are only present in children","Oncogenes are inherited from both parents in every case, so they are found in every cell","Tumour suppressor genes cannot be mutated by any agent"],"e":"Only one allele needs mutating to activate an oncogene, which is far more likely than both alleles of a tumour suppressor being hit. More oncogenes are being discovered all the time. Tumour suppressors are mainly recessive, needing both alleles mutated.","w":["Oncogenes come from normal cellular proto-oncogenes.","Tumour suppressors are present in everyone.","Oncogenes are usually acquired mutations of one allele.","Tumour suppressors can be mutated, e.g. BRCA1/2, TP53, Rb1."],"k":"One-hit activation makes oncogene mutations more common.","rn":[["","How many alleles must be mutated to activate an oncogene?","How many alleles must be mutated to activate an oncogene?","One allele"],["","Why are far fewer tumour suppressor genes known?","Why are far fewer tumour suppressor genes known?","Two alleles less likely mutated"]]},{"id":48030,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"What term describes one mutated tumour suppressor allele giving a lower gene dose that is enough to cause abnormal growth?","a":["haploinsufficiency","haploinsufficient"],"e":"A tumour suppressor should normally need both alleles mutated. With haploinsufficiency, only one allele is mutated: the protein (e.g. a DNA repair protein) still works a little but not as well as it should, because it works at a lower dose. Some abnormal growth still happens, so it can lead to cancer.","k":"Haploinsufficiency = one allele lost → lower dose → abnormal growth.","rn":[["","What is haploinsufficiency?","What is haploinsufficiency?","One TSG allele mutated; Lower gene dose; Still abnormal growth"]]},{"id":48031,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"A cell has one mutated allele of a growth-inducing gene and grows without regulation. Which type of gene and inheritance pattern is this?","o":["Oncogene acting in a dominant manner","Tumour suppressor acting in a recessive manner","Tumour suppressor showing haploinsufficiency","Oncogene acting in a recessive manner","Epigenetic gene acting through methylation"],"e":"In the slide figure, an oncogene (e.g. a growth inducer) with one mutated allele is dominant and causes growth without regulation and abnormal growth. A tumour suppressor growth inhibitor needs both alleles mutated (recessive). A tumour suppressor DNA repair protein with one mutated allele can be haploinsufficient.","w":["A recessive tumour suppressor needs both alleles mutated.","Haploinsufficiency applies to tumour suppressors such as DNA repair proteins, not growth inducers.","Oncogenes act dominantly.","This is a mutation, not an epigenetic change."],"k":"Growth inducer, one allele mutated = dominant oncogene.","rn":[["","Oncogenes act in what manner?","Oncogenes act in what manner?","Dominant"]]},{"id":48032,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"Which statement about the protein product of an oncogene is correct?","o":["It is produced in higher quantities or has increased activity","It is produced in lower quantities and loses its normal growth-controlling activity","It is always deleted from both alleles","It acts only after both alleles have been methylated","It stops growth when the cell is damaged"],"e":"An oncogene mutation results in a gain of function: the protein product is produced in higher quantities, or the altered product has increased activity. This gives the cancer an advantage, so it can grow and proliferate very quickly. The Philadelphia chromosome (BCR-ABL) is an example of increased activity.","w":["Lower quantity and lost activity describe loss of function.","One allele is enough for an oncogene.","Oncogene activation is a mutation, not methylation of both alleles.","Stopping growth is the role of tumour suppressors."],"k":"Oncogene = more protein or more active protein.","rn":[["","What are the 2 ways an oncogene mutation causes gain of function?","What are the 2 ways an oncogene mutation causes gain of function?","Product made in higher quantities; Altered product has increased activity"]]},{"id":48033,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"What is the name for the normal gene that does not lead to cancer but can become an oncogene when mutated?","a":["proto-oncogene","protooncogene","proto oncogene"],"e":"A proto-oncogene is a normal gene that does not lead to cancer development. An oncogene is a proto-oncogene that has been mutated and leads to cancer development (activation). Examples are c-sis (PDGF) becoming v-sis, and c-erbB (EGFR) becoming v-erbB.","k":"Proto-oncogene → (mutation/activation) → oncogene.","rn":[["","What is an oncogene?","What is an oncogene?","Mutated proto-oncogene; Leads to cancer development"],["","What is a proto-oncogene?","What is a proto-oncogene?","Normal gene; Does not lead to cancer"],["","What is the change from proto-oncogene to oncogene called?","What is the change from proto-oncogene to oncogene called?","Activation"]]},{"id":48034,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"The proto-oncogene c-sis makes PDGF (platelet derived growth factor), and the oncogene v-sis makes a truncated version. Why does v-sis promote cancer?","o":["It activates the pathway at inappropriate times, not only in wound healing","It switches off PDGF completely, so wounds cannot heal","It fuses with ABL after a translocation, giving very high tyrosine kinase activity","It removes the extracellular domain of the oestrogen receptor","It methylates the promoter of the c-sis gene"],"e":"c-sis produces PDGF, which is secreted in the wound response and should only be switched on when there is a wound to heal. The oncogene v-sis produces a truncated PDGF located in the cytoplasm, which activates the pathway at inappropriate times, giving extra cell growth and cancer.","w":["It causes activation, not loss.","Fusion with ABL describes BCR-ABL.","Loss of an extracellular domain describes v-erbB (EGFR).","v-sis is a mutated gene, not a methylation event."],"k":"v-sis: truncated PDGF active at the wrong times.","rn":[["","What does the v-sis product do wrongly?","What does the v-sis product do wrongly?","Activates pathway at inappropriate times"]]},{"id":48035,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"The oncogene v-erbB encodes a truncated EGFR (epidermal growth factor receptor) with its extracellular domain deleted. What is the consequence?","o":["The receptor signals all the time without needing a ligand","The receptor can no longer signal even when ligand binds","The receptor binds extra ligand and signals only in wounds","The receptor is secreted and acts in the wound response","The receptor moves to the nucleus as a transcription factor"],"e":"c-erbB produces EGFR, important for normal cell signalling, growth and development. v-erbB is a truncated EGFR with the extracellular domain deleted. That domain is normally where a ligand binds to make the receptor signal for growth and proliferation; without it the receptor needs no stimulus and works on its own, all the time.","w":["The truncated receptor is active, not inactive.","Without the extracellular domain it cannot bind ligand.","Secretion in the wound response describes PDGF.","Myc is the transcription factor oncogene."],"k":"v-erbB: no extracellular domain → constant signalling.","rn":[["","Why does v-erbB EGFR signal without a stimulus?","Why does v-erbB EGFR signal without a stimulus?","No extracellular domain; Works on its own"]]},{"id":48036,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"Which pairing of proto-oncogene with its normal protein is correct?","o":["c-erbB → EGFR (epidermal growth factor receptor)","c-sis → EGFR (epidermal growth factor receptor)","c-erbB → PDGF (platelet derived growth factor)","c-sis → VEGF (vascular endothelial growth factor)","c-erbB → BCR-ABL fusion protein"],"e":"The proto-oncogene c-sis produces platelet derived growth factor (PDGF), secreted in the wound response. The proto-oncogene c-erbB produces the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR). Their viral oncogene forms are v-sis (truncated PDGF) and v-erbB (truncated EGFR).","w":["c-sis produces PDGF.","c-erbB produces EGFR.","VEGF is the angiogenesis factor.","BCR-ABL comes from the 9;22 translocation."],"k":"c-sis = PDGF; c-erbB = EGFR.","rn":[["","Proto-oncogene c-erbB produces which protein?","Proto-oncogene c-erbB produces which protein?","EGFR (epidermal growth factor receptor)"]]},{"id":48037,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"In which cellular location is the truncated PDGF produced by v-sis found?","a":["cytoplasm","the cytoplasm"],"e":"The oncogene v-sis produces a truncated (shortened) version of PDGF that is located in the cytoplasm. It activates the pathway at inappropriate times, not just in wound healing, giving extra cell growth and cancer. Normal PDGF from c-sis is secreted in the wound response.","k":"v-sis PDGF is cytoplasmic.","rn":[["","Where is the v-sis product located?","Where is the v-sis product located?","Cytoplasm"]]},{"id":48038,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"In which three cancers are RAS mutations described as highly prevalent?","o":["Lung, colorectal and pancreatic","Breast, ovarian and prostate","Lung, breast and leukaemia","Cervical, colorectal and skin","Retinoblastoma, pancreatic and ovarian"],"e":"K-Ras encodes a protein in the Ras/MAP kinase pathway, and RAS mutations are highly prevalent in lung, colorectal and pancreatic cancers. H-Ras and N-Ras mutations also occur. Breast, ovarian and prostate cancers are linked with BRCA1/2.","w":["These are the BRCA-linked cancers.","Breast cancer and leukaemia are not the cancers listed for RAS.","Cervical and skin cancers are not listed for RAS.","Retinoblastoma is linked with Rb, and ovarian with BRCA."],"k":"RAS: lung, colorectal, pancreatic.","rn":[["","RAS mutations are highly prevalent in which 3 cancers?","RAS mutations are highly prevalent in which 3 cancers?","Lung; Colorectal; Pancreatic"]]},{"id":48039,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"Which oncogene encodes a transcription factor that promotes proliferation by controlling target genes including N-Ras and p53?","a":["myc","c-myc"],"e":"Myc encodes a transcription factor protein, a protein that switches other genes on. It promotes proliferation by controlling the expression of target genes, including N-Ras and p53. In the slide pathway, Myc sits downstream of Ras-Raf and connects to telomerase, cell-cycle proteins and p53.","k":"Myc = transcription factor oncogene.","rn":[["","Myc encodes what type of protein?","Myc encodes what type of protein?","Transcription factor"]]},{"id":48040,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"K-Ras encodes a protein in which signalling pathway?","o":["The Ras/MAP kinase (mitogen-activated protein kinase) pathway","The VEGF/VEGFR2 (vascular endothelial growth factor receptor 2) angiogenesis pathway","The oestrogen receptor pathway","The PARP DNA repair pathway","The telomerase pathway"],"e":"K-Ras encodes a protein expressed in the Ras/MAP kinase pathway. RAS mutations are highly prevalent in lung, colorectal and pancreatic cancers, and H-Ras and N-Ras mutations also occur. In the Myc figure, Ras-Raf feeds into Myc.","w":["VEGF/VEGFR2 is the angiogenesis pathway.","The oestrogen receptor relates to hormone-driven cancers.","PARP inhibitors target genome instability.","Telomerase relates to immortality downstream of Myc."],"k":"K-Ras acts in the Ras/MAP kinase pathway.","rn":[["","K-Ras encodes a protein in which pathway?","K-Ras encodes a protein in which pathway?","Ras/MAP kinase pathway"]]},{"id":48041,"t":"Targeting the Hallmarks","s":"In the Myc signalling figure, which output of Myc leads to immortalization?","o":["Telomerase","p19ARF","Cdc25A (cell division cycle 25A)","p27","CyclinE-Cdk2"],"e":"In the figure, Myc outputs to telomerase, leading to immortalization. p27 (blocked) and Cdc25A feed CyclinE-Cdk2, leading to proliferation. p19ARF feeds p53, leading to apoptosis.","w":["p19ARF leads to p53 and apoptosis.","Cdc25A leads to CyclinE-Cdk2 and proliferation.","p27 is blocked on the path to proliferation.","CyclinE-Cdk2 leads to proliferation."],"k":"Myc → telomerase → immortalization.","rn":[["","What do telomerase inhibitors block?","What do telomerase inhibitors block?","Replicative immortality"]]},{"id":48042,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"What term describes a cell population obtaining the ability to multiply indefinitely?","a":["immortalisation","immortalization","immortality","replicative immortality"],"e":"Immortalisation is when a cell population obtains the ability to multiply indefinitely. It is very bad in the body, because it gives a tumour, but handy in cell culture. In the Myc pathway, telomerase leads to immortalization, and \"enabling replicative immortality\" is one of the original hallmarks.","k":"Immortalisation = indefinite multiplication.","rn":[["","What is immortalisation?","What is immortalisation?","Cell population multiplies indefinitely"]]},{"id":48043,"t":"Assays of Oncogenic Transformation","s":"DNA from chemically transformed mouse fibroblasts is put into normal mouse fibroblasts, which then form a focus of transformed cells; injected into a mouse, they form a tumour. What does this experiment show?","o":["The transformed cells' DNA contains oncogenes that can cause cancer","Transformation depends only on the chemical that was added, not on the DNA itself","Normal fibroblasts cannot be transformed by any DNA","Cancer is caused only by viruses carried in the DNA","Tumours form only when both alleles of a suppressor are lost"],"e":"In the experiment, DNA is extracted from chemically transformed mouse fibroblasts and transfected (by calcium phosphate co-precipitation) into normal mouse fibroblasts. These form a focus of morphologically transformed cells, and when injected into a mouse host they form a tumour. The conclusion is that the transformed DNA causes cancer, so oncogenes are in the DNA.","w":["The DNA alone transformed the normal cells.","The normal fibroblasts were transformed.","The DNA came from chemically transformed cells.","The experiment shows a dominant DNA effect, not loss of a suppressor."],"k":"DNA transfection shows oncogenes are in cancer DNA.","rn":[["","What does the oncogene detection experiment prove?","What does the oncogene detection experiment prove?","Transformed DNA causes cancer"],["","In cancer, which way do oncogenes, tumour suppressors and DNA integrity change?","In cancer, which way do oncogenes, tumour suppressors and DNA integrity change?","Oncogenes up; Tumour suppressors down; DNA integrity down"]]},{"id":48044,"t":"Assays of Oncogenic Transformation","s":"What term means putting DNA into cells, as in the oncogene detection experiment?","a":["transfection"],"e":"Transfection means putting DNA into cells. In the oncogene detection experiment, DNA from transformed mouse fibroblasts is transfected into normal fibroblasts using a calcium phosphate co-precipitation procedure. The cells become transformed and form tumours in mice.","k":"Transfection = putting DNA into cells."},{"id":48045,"t":"Assays of Oncogenic Transformation","s":"Which method is used to transfect DNA into normal mouse fibroblasts in the oncogene detection experiment?","o":["Calcium phosphate co-precipitation","Trypsin dissociation into single cells","Giemsa staining","Methanol fixation","Chromosome painting"],"e":"The figure shows chemically transformed mouse fibroblasts, their DNA extracted, then transfection using a calcium phosphate co-precipitation procedure into normal mouse fibroblasts. Trypsin, Giemsa and methanol belong to the hamster embryo transformation assay, and chromosome painting to karyotyping.","w":["Trypsin dissociates embryo cells in the transformation assay.","Giemsa stains colonies in the transformation assay.","Methanol fixes cells before Giemsa staining.","Chromosome painting is used to see translocations."],"k":"Transfection by calcium phosphate co-precipitation."},{"id":48046,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"A man carries a mutated BRCA2 gene. Which cancers is he at increased risk of?","o":["Breast and prostate","Breast and ovarian","Lung and pancreatic","Retinoblastoma and leukaemia","Colorectal and lung"],"e":"In their mutated forms, BRCA1 and BRCA2 play a role in breast and ovarian cancers in women and increase the risk of breast and prostate cancer in men. Male breast cancer risk is very small but real.","w":["Breast and ovarian is the pattern in women.","Lung and pancreatic cancers are linked with RAS.","Retinoblastoma is linked with Rb.","These are not the cancers listed for BRCA in men."],"k":"BRCA in men: breast and prostate.","rn":[["","Mutated BRCA1/2 raise the risk of which cancers in men?","Mutated BRCA1/2 raise the risk of which cancers in men?","Breast; Prostate"]]},{"id":48047,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"A woman with a strong family history of breast and ovarian cancer chooses removal of both breasts and her ovaries. Which statement best explains this choice?","o":["She may never get cancer, but her inherited risk factors are very high","She already has proven invasive cancer in both organs","BRCA genes are oncogenes that are certain to cause cancer in every carrier","Surgery removes the BRCA gene from all her cells","Removal is required by law for all BRCA carriers"],"e":"The lecture describes a woman with cancer in her family who had a double mastectomy and her ovaries removed to limit her very high risk. She might never have got cancer, but the risk factors were very high. Mutated BRCA1 and BRCA2 play a role in breast and ovarian cancers in women.","w":["The surgery was preventive.","BRCA1/2 are tumour suppressors, and cancer is not certain.","Surgery removes at-risk tissue, not the gene from every cell.","It is a personal choice, not a legal requirement."],"k":"Preventive surgery reduces a high, not certain, risk.","rn":[["","Which hormone factor raises cancer risk?","Which hormone factor raises cancer risk?","High oestrogen levels"],["","What are the inherited gene examples of cancer risk?","What are the inherited gene examples of cancer risk?","BRCA1; BRCA2"]]},{"id":48048,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"A patient with a family history of cancer asks how to find out whether they carry a cancer risk gene. What route is described?","o":["Referral from the GP (general practitioner) to a local genetics service","Direct referral for immediate preventive surgery","A mammogram to detect the inherited gene","A histology biopsy of normal breast tissue to look for the inherited mutation","An immunotherapy trial to test for the gene"],"e":"For predictive genetic tests for cancer risk genes, a person may get a referral from the GP to a local genetics service, particularly if there is already a family history. Some people want to know and some may not, so this must be discussed with patients.","w":["Testing and counselling come before any surgery.","Mammograms image breast tissue, not genes.","Biopsy is not the route described.","Immunotherapy is a treatment, not a genetic test."],"k":"Predictive testing: GP → genetics service.","rn":[["","Where does the GP refer for predictive genetic tests?","Where does the GP refer for predictive genetic tests?","Local genetics service"],["","When is referral for predictive genetic tests most likely?","When is referral for predictive genetic tests most likely?","Family history of cancer"]]},{"id":48049,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"Why must predictive genetic testing for cancer risk genes be discussed carefully with each patient?","o":["Some people want to know their risk, but others may not","The test result is always certain to predict cancer","Testing is only possible after cancer has developed","The test changes the patient's DNA sequence","A positive result means no treatment is available"],"e":"Predictive genetic testing raises an ethical question: do people want to know? Some do (\"am I a ticking time bomb?\") and some may not. Knowledge is power, but it must be discussed with patients.","w":["A mutated gene raises likelihood but is not certain.","Predictive tests are done before cancer develops.","Testing does not change DNA.","Preventive options exist, e.g. surgery."],"k":"Genetic testing needs discussion: not everyone wants to know.","rn":[["","What must be discussed before predictive gene testing?","What must be discussed before predictive gene testing?","Whether they want to know"]]},{"id":48050,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"Which gene codes for the retinoblastoma protein?","a":["rb1","rb","rb 1"],"e":"Rb1 codes for the retinoblastoma protein, Rb (pRb). Mutations lead to retinoblastoma, a really rare cancer of the eye in very young children. pRb is a tumour suppressor that, with p53, gives the growth inhibitory signal at the restriction point in G1.","k":"Rb1 → retinoblastoma protein.","rn":[["","Rb1 codes for what?","Rb1 codes for what?","Retinoblastoma protein (pRb)"]]},{"id":48051,"t":"Cell Cycle and Cancer","s":"In the cell-cycle figure, where do the tumour suppressors p53 and pRb give their growth inhibitory signal?","o":["At the restriction point (R) in G1","At the start of S phase as DNA is copied","During mitosis at the M checkpoint","During G2 before mitosis","During cytokinesis"],"e":"In the slide, oncogenes (Ras, Myc) give the proliferation signal into G1. Tumour suppressors (p53 and pRb) give the growth inhibitory signal at R, the restriction point in G1, with a line to apoptosis. Genomic stability genes protect DNA integrity in S.","w":["S phase is where genomic stability (DNA integrity) acts.","The figure places p53 and pRb at R in G1.","The figure places p53 and pRb at R in G1.","Cytokinesis is not where these genes act in the figure."],"k":"p53 and pRb act at the restriction point in G1.","rn":[["","Where do P53 and pRb act in the cell cycle?","Where do P53 and pRb act in the cell cycle?","Restriction point (R); In G1"]]},{"id":48052,"t":"Cell Cycle and Cancer","s":"Which set of changes does the slide key show for cancer cells?","o":["Oncogenes up, tumour suppressors down, DNA integrity down","Oncogenes down, tumour suppressors up, DNA integrity up","Oncogenes up, tumour suppressors up, DNA integrity down","Oncogenes down, tumour suppressors down, DNA integrity up","Oncogenes up, tumour suppressors down, DNA integrity up"],"e":"The key on the cell-cycle slide shows that in cancer, oncogenes are up, tumour suppressors are down and DNA integrity is down. With these mutations, cells go on to immortalisation, transformation and metastasis.","w":["This is the reverse of the key.","Tumour suppressors are down.","Oncogenes are up and DNA integrity is down.","DNA integrity is down in cancer."],"k":"Cancer: oncogenes ↑, tumour suppressors ↓, DNA integrity ↓.","rn":[["","In cancer, which way do oncogenes, tumour suppressors and DNA integrity change?","In cancer, which way do oncogenes, tumour suppressors and DNA integrity change?","Oncogenes up; Tumour suppressors down; DNA integrity down"]]},{"id":48053,"t":"Cell Cycle and Cancer","s":"In which cell-cycle phase do genomic stability genes, such as BRCA1 and BRCA2, protect DNA integrity?","a":["s","s phase","synthesis","synthesis phase"],"e":"In the cell-cycle figure, genomic stability (DNA integrity) acts at S, the synthesis phase in which DNA replicates. Genomic stability genes such as BRCA1 and BRCA2 protect DNA integrity; if mutated, DNA integrity is compromised.","k":"S phase: BRCA1/2 protect DNA integrity.","rn":[["","Which genes act at genomic stability in S phase?","Which genes act at genomic stability in S phase?","BRCA1; BRCA2"],["","BRCA1 and BRCA2 normally protect what?","BRCA1 and BRCA2 normally protect what?","Genome stability"]]},{"id":48054,"t":"Cell Cycle and Cancer","s":"A cell has mutations in Ras, p53 and BRCA1. According to the cell-cycle figure, what sequence of changes follows?","o":["Immortalisation → transformation → metastasis","Transformation → immortalisation → apoptosis","Metastasis → immortalisation → transformation","Apoptosis → immortalisation → metastasis","Contact inhibition → transformation → apoptosis"],"e":"The figure shows a normal cell changing through immortalisation, then transformation, then metastasis. Oncogenes (Ras, Myc) are up, tumour suppressors (p53, pRb) are down and DNA integrity (BRCA1/2) is down.","w":["Immortalisation comes before transformation, and apoptosis is avoided.","Metastasis is the end point.","Apoptosis is avoided by these cells.","Contact inhibition is lost, not gained."],"k":"Normal → immortalisation → transformation → metastasis."},{"id":48055,"t":"Cell Cycle and Cancer","s":"Which genes give the proliferation signal into G1 in the cell-cycle figure?","o":["Ras and Myc","p53 and pRb","BRCA1 and BRCA2","p16 and p27","VEGF and VEGFR2"],"e":"In the cell-cycle figure, a big arrow labelled \"Oncogenes: Ras, Myc (proliferation signal)\" pushes into G1. Tumour suppressors p53 and pRb give the growth inhibitory signal at the restriction point, and BRCA1/2 protect DNA integrity at S.","w":["p53 and pRb give the growth inhibitory signal.","BRCA1/2 protect DNA integrity.","These are not shown giving the proliferation signal.","VEGF and VEGFR2 drive angiogenesis."],"k":"Ras and Myc = proliferation signal in G1.","rn":[["","In which phase do Ras and Myc signal?","In which phase do Ras and Myc signal?","G1"]]},{"id":48056,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"Which statement best describes an epigenetic change?","o":["It changes the phenotype but not the genotype","It changes the genotype but not the phenotype","It changes the order of the DNA bases","It always deletes a piece of a chromosome","It occurs only in inherited cancers"],"e":"Epigenetic means \"upon\" the genome: alterations in gene expression encoded by modifications of the genome and its chromatin components, but not by changes in the DNA sequence. So it changes phenotype (what the cell is like) but not genotype (the DNA code).","w":["This is the reverse.","The DNA sequence is unchanged.","Deletions are genetic changes.","Epigenetic changes are affected by environment, diet, ageing and more."],"k":"Epigenetic: phenotype changes, genotype does not.","rn":[["","Epigenetic changes alter phenotype or genotype?","Epigenetic changes alter phenotype or genotype?","Phenotype only"]]},{"id":48057,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"Which three epigenetic mechanisms in cancer are listed?","o":["Histone acetylation, histone methylation and DNA methylation","DNA deletion, DNA translocation and DNA methylation of tumour suppressor promoters","Histone acetylation, point mutation and translocation","DNA replication, DNA repair and DNA methylation","Histone methylation, transfection and point mutation"],"e":"The three epigenetic mechanisms in cancer listed (Cheng et al., 2019) are histone acetylation, histone methylation and DNA methylation. Deletions, translocations and point mutations are genetic changes to the DNA sequence.","w":["Deletions and translocations are genetic changes.","Point mutations and translocations are genetic.","Replication and repair are genome-stability processes.","Transfection is a laboratory technique, and point mutation is genetic."],"k":"Epigenetic: histone acetylation, histone methylation, DNA methylation.","rn":[["","What are the 3 epigenetic mechanisms in cancer?","What are the 3 epigenetic mechanisms in cancer?","Histone acetylation; Histone methylation; DNA methylation"]]},{"id":48058,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"DNA is wound tightly around histones in one region and loosely in another. Which region is likely to contain active genes?","o":["The loosely wound region, because the DNA is accessible","The tightly wound region, because the DNA there is protected from damage","Both regions equally, because the sequence is the same","Neither region, because histones block all genes","The tightly wound region, because it is methylated"],"e":"Histones are proteins around which DNA winds for compaction and gene regulation. Tightly wound DNA is inaccessible and the gene is inactive; loosened DNA is accessible and the gene is active. Epigenetic factors binding to histone tails alter how tightly DNA is wrapped and whether genes can be activated.","w":["Tightly wound DNA is inaccessible and inactive.","Accessibility, not sequence, decides activity here.","Loosened DNA is accessible and active.","Tight winding makes genes inactive."],"k":"Loose chromatin = accessible = active."},{"id":48059,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"What is the name for the proteins around which DNA winds for compaction and gene regulation?","a":["histones","histone"],"e":"Histones are proteins around which DNA winds for compaction and gene regulation. Histone modifications (acetylation and methylation) by epigenetic factors binding to histone tails alter how tightly DNA is wrapped and whether genes can be activated.","k":"Histones package DNA and regulate genes.","rn":[["","What are histones?","What are histones?","Proteins DNA winds around; For compaction and gene regulation"]]},{"id":48060,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"Which factors are shown on the slide as affecting epigenetic mechanisms?","o":["Development, environmental chemicals, drugs, ageing and diet","Blood group, height, eye colour, handedness, sex and the number of siblings","Translocations, deletions, point mutations and insertions","Tumour grade, stage, size and lymph node status","Oestrogen receptor, HER2, EGFR and RAS status only"],"e":"The epigenetics slide shows that epigenetic mechanisms are affected by development (in utero, childhood), environmental chemicals, drugs/pharmaceuticals, ageing and diet. Health endpoints include cancer, autoimmune disease, mental disorders and diabetes.","w":["These traits are not listed.","These are genetic changes.","These are pathological features of a tumour.","These are molecular markers, not influences on epigenetics."],"k":"Epigenetics is affected by development, chemicals, drugs, ageing, diet.","rn":[["","Besides development, which 4 factors affect epigenetic mechanisms?","Besides development, which 4 factors affect epigenetic mechanisms?","Environmental chemicals; Drugs/pharmaceuticals; Aging; Diet"]]},{"id":48061,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"When in tumour formation do DNA methylation alterations typically occur?","o":["As early events in tumorigenesis","Only after distant metastasis has occurred","Only once the tumour has been treated","Only after both tumour suppressor alleles are deleted","Only in benign tumours that never progress"],"e":"DNA methylation alterations are early events in tumorigenesis (tumour formation). They are important in the regulation of gene expression in cancer cells. Specific methylation profiles are seen with mutated genes involved in lung cancer.","w":["They are described as early, not late.","Timing in relation to treatment is not described.","Methylation can silence genes without any deletion.","They are linked to cancer development, not only benign tumours."],"k":"DNA methylation changes are early in tumorigenesis.","rn":[["","When do DNA methylation alterations occur in tumorigenesis?","When do DNA methylation alterations occur in tumorigenesis?","Early events"]]},{"id":48062,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"Specific methylation profiles have been seen with which mutated genes in lung cancer?","o":["EGFR, KRAS and TP53","BRCA1, BRCA2 and Rb1","BCR, ABL and Myc","c-sis, v-sis and PDGF","HER2, ER and VEGFA"],"e":"Specific methylation profiles are seen with mutated genes involved in lung cancer: EGFR, KRAS and TP53 mutations (Bjaanaes et al., 2016). This shows that epigenetic and genetic changes are linked in cancer.","w":["These are inherited/tumour suppressor genes not given for this study.","BCR-ABL is linked with CML.","These relate to the PDGF oncogene example.","These are not the genes given for the lung study."],"k":"Lung cancer methylation profiles: EGFR, KRAS, TP53.","rn":[["","Specific methylation profiles are seen with which lung cancer mutations?","Specific methylation profiles are seen with which lung cancer mutations?","EGFR; KRAS; TP53"]]},{"id":48063,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"A tumour has a completely normal DNA sequence in a key growth gene, yet the gene is expressed in a tissue where it should be silent. How can this lead to cancer?","o":["Faulty control of gene expression by molecules binding the DNA can drive growth","The normal sequence must contain a hidden translocation that sequencing has missed in this gene","Only mutations in the sequence can lead to cancer","The gene must have been transfected from a virus","Its expression shows the tumour is benign"],"e":"In epigenetics, molecules bind to the DNA and either stop or repress certain genes, or cause genes to be expressed where they shouldn't be. So even with normal DNA, faulty gene-expression control can lead to cancer. This was only really understood in the last 20–30 years; before, people thought DNA mutations were all that caused cancer.","w":["Expression can change without any sequence change.","Epigenetic changes can also cause cancer.","No viral source is needed.","Abnormal expression does not indicate a benign tumour."],"k":"Normal DNA + wrong expression can still cause cancer.","rn":[["","How do epigenetic molecules alter genes?","How do epigenetic molecules alter genes?","Bind DNA; Repress or wrongly express genes"]]},{"id":48064,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"What does the Greek prefix \"epi\" in epigenetics mean?","a":["upon","on","above","over"],"e":"Epigenetic is Greek for \"upon\" the genome. It refers to alterations in gene expression encoded by modifications of the genome and its chromatin components, but not by changes in the DNA sequence.","k":"Epi = upon.","rn":[["","What does \"epi\" mean in Greek?","What does \"epi\" mean in Greek?","Upon (the genome)"]]},{"id":48065,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"What is the term for DNA plus its packing proteins?","a":["chromatin"],"e":"Chromatin is DNA plus the proteins it is packed with. Epigenetic changes are alterations in gene expression encoded by modifications of the genome and its chromatin components. On the slide, the chromosome unwinds into chromatin and then DNA.","k":"Chromatin = DNA + packing proteins.","rn":[["","What are epigenetic changes?","What are epigenetic changes?","Altered gene expression; Genome/chromatin modifications; Not DNA sequence changes"]]},{"id":48066,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"Which of the following is one of the six original hallmarks of cancer (Hanahan and Weinberg, 2000)?","o":["Inducing angiogenesis","Avoiding immune destruction","Deregulating cellular energetics","Genome instability and mutation","Tumour-promoting inflammation"],"e":"The six original hallmarks are sustaining proliferative signalling, evading growth suppressors, activating invasion and metastasis, enabling replicative immortality, inducing angiogenesis and resisting cell death. In 2011, avoiding immune destruction and deregulating cellular energetics were added as emerging hallmarks, and genome instability and mutation and tumour-promoting inflammation as enabling characteristics.","w":["This is a 2011 emerging hallmark.","This is a 2011 emerging hallmark.","This is a 2011 enabling characteristic.","This is a 2011 enabling characteristic."],"k":"Six originals include inducing angiogenesis.","rn":[["","What is the key growth factor in angiogenesis?","What is the key growth factor in angiogenesis?","VEGF"],["","When should angiogenesis normally happen?","When should angiogenesis normally happen?","Wound healing; Embryonic development"]]},{"id":48067,"t":"Targeting the Hallmarks","s":"Which of the following is NOT one of the six original hallmarks of cancer?","o":["Avoiding immune destruction","Sustaining proliferative signalling","Evading growth suppressors","Enabling replicative immortality","Resisting cell death"],"e":"The six original hallmarks are sustaining proliferative signalling, evading growth suppressors, activating invasion and metastasis, enabling replicative immortality, inducing angiogenesis and resisting cell death. Avoiding immune destruction was added in 2011 as an emerging hallmark.","w":["This is an original hallmark.","This is an original hallmark.","This is an original hallmark.","This is an original hallmark."],"k":"Avoiding immune destruction is a 2011 emerging hallmark.","rn":[["","How do cancer cells avoid the immune system?","How do cancer cells avoid the immune system?","Change expressed surface molecules"]]},{"id":48068,"t":"Targeting the Hallmarks","s":"A tumour's cells keep dividing well beyond the set number of divisions normal body cells undergo before dying and being reabsorbed. Which hallmark does this represent?","o":["Enabling replicative immortality","Sustaining proliferative signalling","Inducing angiogenesis","Evading growth suppressors","Activating invasion and metastasis"],"e":"Body cells should have a set number of divisions, then die and be reabsorbed. Enabling replicative immortality means cells just keep growing past this limit. Telomerase inhibitors target this hallmark, because in cancer the chromosome caps do not degrade over time as they normally would.","w":["This is cells continuing to receive growth signals.","This is making new blood vessels.","This is avoiding molecules that stop growth.","This is breaking out and spreading."],"k":"Exceeding the division limit = replicative immortality.","rn":[["","What do telomerase inhibitors block?","What do telomerase inhibitors block?","Replicative immortality"]]},{"id":48069,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"Which original hallmark is described as malignant disease breaking out of the basement membrane and spreading around the body?","a":["activating invasion and metastasis","invasion and metastasis","invasion","metastasis"],"e":"Activating invasion and metastasis is malignant disease breaking out of the basement membrane and spreading around the body. Lazebnik (2010) noted it seems to be the only original hallmark that distinguishes between malignant and benign tumours. Inhibitors of HGF/c-Met target it.","k":"Invasion and metastasis = breaking out and spreading.","rn":[["","What does activating invasion and metastasis involve?","What does activating invasion and metastasis involve?","Breaking the basement membrane; Spreading around body"]]},{"id":48070,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"Which hallmark means avoiding programmed cell death (apoptosis)?","o":["Resisting cell death","Evading growth suppressors","Enabling replicative immortality","Avoiding immune destruction","Deregulating cellular energetics"],"e":"Resisting cell death means avoiding programmed cell death (apoptosis). It is targeted by pro-apoptotic BH3 mimetics. Evading growth suppressors means avoiding molecules that stop the cancer from growing.","w":["This is avoiding growth-stopping molecules.","This is continuing to divide past the normal limit.","This is escaping the immune system.","This is altered energy metabolism."],"k":"Resisting cell death = avoiding apoptosis.","rn":[["","Resisting cell death means avoiding what?","Resisting cell death means avoiding what?","Apoptosis"]]},{"id":48071,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"What is the definition of angiogenesis?","a":["formation of new blood vessels from pre-existing ones","formation of new blood vessels","new blood vessel formation","growth of new blood vessels"],"e":"Angiogenesis is the formation of new blood vessels from pre-existing ones. Normally it is important in the embryo, so the circulation can develop, and in wound healing; adults should have little going on. Cancer cells take over this process, and inducing angiogenesis is one of the original hallmarks.","k":"Angiogenesis = new vessels from existing vessels.","rn":[["","What is angiogenesis?","What is angiogenesis?","New blood vessels; From pre-existing ones"]]},{"id":48072,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"A rapidly growing tumour outstrips its blood supply. Which sequence describes how new vessels are recruited?","o":["Hypoxic cells → secrete VEGFA → activate VEGFR2 → new vessel growth","Hypoxic cells → secrete PDGF → activate EGFR → new vessel growth","Well-oxygenated cells → secrete VEGFR2 → bind VEGFA → vessel regression","Hypoxic cells → secrete CTLA4 → activate T cells → new vessel growth","Hypoxic cells → activate telomerase → immortalise endothelium → new vessels"],"e":"Cancer cells grow so fast they become hypoxic (short of oxygen and nutrients). Hypoxic cancer cells secrete VEGFA; VEGF is a key growth factor in angiogenesis. VEGFA activates VEGF receptor 2 (VEGFR2), leading to new blood vessel growth.","w":["PDGF and EGFR are not the angiogenesis factors described.","The cells are hypoxic, VEGFR2 is a receptor, and vessels grow.","CTLA4 relates to the immune system.","Telomerase relates to immortality."],"k":"Hypoxia → VEGFA → VEGFR2 → angiogenesis.","rn":[["","Which cancer cells secrete VEGFA?","Which cancer cells secrete VEGFA?","Hypoxic cancer cells"],["","Which receptor does VEGFA activate?","Which receptor does VEGFA activate?","VEGFR2"]]},{"id":48073,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"Which receptor does VEGFA activate to drive new blood vessel growth in tumours?","a":["vegfr2","vegf receptor 2","vascular endothelial growth factor receptor 2","vegfr-2"],"e":"Hypoxic cancer cells secrete VEGFA, which activates VEGF receptor 2 (VEGFR2), leading to new blood vessel growth. Inhibitors of VEGF signalling target the hallmark of inducing angiogenesis.","k":"VEGFA → VEGFR2.","rn":[["","Which receptor does VEGFA activate?","Which receptor does VEGFA activate?","VEGFR2"],["","What does VEGFR2 activation lead to?","What does VEGFR2 activation lead to?","New blood vessel growth"],["","What does VEGFR2 stand for?","What does VEGFR2 stand for?","VEGF receptor 2"]]},{"id":48074,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"Vessels in normal tissue run in neat, roughly parallel lines, but tumour vessels form a dense, tangled mesh. What explains this?","o":["Tumour vessels grow quickly and uncontrollably, like the cancer cells","Tumour vessels are older than normal vessels and have been damaged over a long time","Tumour vessels are formed only in the embryo","Normal vessels lose their organisation during wound healing","Tumour vessels are made by lymphocytes rather than endothelium"],"e":"The slide compares blood vessels in normal tissue (well organised) and in a tumour (chaotic). Tumour vessels grow quickly and uncontrollably, like the cells, producing a dense tangled mesh. The lecturer's undergraduate project labelled blood vessels with antibodies in normal, benign and tumour tissue.","w":["The chaos comes from rapid uncontrolled growth, not age.","Tumours induce new vessel growth.","Normal tissue vessels are well organised.","This is not described."],"k":"Tumour vessels are chaotic; normal ones are organised.","rn":[["","Why are tumour blood vessels chaotic?","Why are tumour blood vessels chaotic?","Grow quickly, uncontrollably"]]},{"id":48075,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"Why are angiogenesis inhibitors given only for a limited period?","o":["The patient needs to heal properly after any injury","They become toxic to the tumour's DNA repair","They switch on oncogenes in normal cells after prolonged use","They cause the tumour to become benign","They block oestrogen receptors in all tissues"],"e":"Stopping the angiogenesis molecule stops angiogenesis. These drugs are only given for so long, because the patient needs to heal properly after any injury, and wound healing normally needs angiogenesis. The slide labels this drug class as inhibitors of VEGF signalling.","w":["This reason is not given.","This is not described.","Drugs do not make tumours benign.","Oestrogen receptors are targeted by tamoxifen."],"k":"Angiogenesis inhibitors limited because wounds need vessels."},{"id":48076,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"Why can a cancer not invade effectively without inducing angiogenesis?","o":["It needs a good blood, oxygen and nutrient supply","Angiogenesis directly destroys the basement membrane","New vessels switch off all growth suppressors","Angiogenesis converts the tumour into a lymphoma","New vessels are needed to make telomerase"],"e":"Inducing angiogenesis means making new blood vessels. A cancer cannot invade without a good blood, oxygen and nutrient supply. Fast-growing cancer cells become hypoxic and secrete VEGFA to recruit new vessels.","w":["This mechanism is not described.","Vessels supply the tumour; they do not switch off suppressors.","Lymphomas arise from the lymphatic system.","Telomerase is produced by the cancer cells, not by vessels."],"k":"Invasion needs a blood supply.","rn":[["","Why must a cancer induce angiogenesis to invade?","Why must a cancer induce angiogenesis to invade?","Blood supply; Oxygen; Nutrients"]]},{"id":48077,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"Which two were added in 2011 as emerging hallmarks?","o":["Deregulating cellular energetics and avoiding immune destruction","Genome instability and tumour-promoting inflammation","Inducing angiogenesis and resisting cell death","Sustaining proliferative signalling and evading growth suppressors","Avoiding immune destruction and genome instability"],"e":"In 2011 Hanahan and Weinberg added emerging hallmarks (deregulating cellular energetics and avoiding immune destruction) and enabling characteristics (genome instability and mutation, and tumour-promoting inflammation). The original six still stand.","w":["These are enabling characteristics.","These are original hallmarks.","These are original hallmarks.","Genome instability is an enabling characteristic."],"k":"Emerging: energetics + immune evasion.","rn":[["","Why is deregulating cellular energetics a hallmark?","Why is deregulating cellular energetics a hallmark?","Cells need energy to grow; Metabolism"]]},{"id":48078,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"Which pair are the enabling characteristics added in 2011?","o":["Genome instability and mutation; tumour-promoting inflammation","Deregulating cellular energetics; avoiding immune destruction by the body's T cells","Inducing angiogenesis; activating invasion and metastasis","Enabling replicative immortality; resisting cell death","Avoiding immune destruction; tumour-promoting inflammation"],"e":"The enabling characteristics are genome instability and mutation, and tumour-promoting inflammation. They are not full hallmarks; they enable the cancer to form. The emerging hallmarks are deregulating cellular energetics and avoiding immune destruction.","w":["These are emerging hallmarks.","These are original hallmarks.","These are original hallmarks.","Avoiding immune destruction is an emerging hallmark."],"k":"Enabling: genome instability + inflammation.","rn":[["","What is the example of tumour-promoting inflammation?","What is the example of tumour-promoting inflammation?","Particulates irritating lungs"],["","Which drugs counter tumour-promoting inflammation?","Which drugs counter tumour-promoting inflammation?","Anti-inflammatory drugs"]]},{"id":48079,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"What is the difference between an enabling characteristic and a hallmark of cancer?","o":["An enabling characteristic is not a full hallmark but enables the cancer to form","An enabling characteristic is found only in benign tumours","An enabling characteristic was described in 2000, hallmarks in 2011","An enabling characteristic cannot be targeted by any drug, so it is of no use in treatment","An enabling characteristic is a type of staging system"],"e":"Enabling characteristics (genome instability and mutation, tumour-promoting inflammation) are not full hallmarks; they enable the cancer to form. For example, there are no cancers without mutations and loss of DNA integrity. They can still be targeted, e.g. PARP inhibitors for genome instability and selective anti-inflammatory drugs for inflammation.","w":["They enable cancer to form.","The originals were 2000; enabling characteristics were added in 2011.","They can be targeted, e.g. by PARP inhibitors.","They are not staging systems."],"k":"Enabling characteristics enable cancer but are not hallmarks.","rn":[["","Enabling characteristics vs hallmarks","Enabling characteristics vs hallmarks","Not full hallmarks; Enable cancer to form"]]},{"id":48080,"t":"Targeting the Hallmarks","s":"A patient on long-term immunosuppression after a transplant develops cancer. Which emerging hallmark is most relevant to their increased risk?","o":["Avoiding immune destruction","Deregulating cellular energetics","Inducing angiogenesis","Enabling replicative immortality","Sustaining proliferative signalling"],"e":"The immune system can kill a few cancer cells, and cancer takes hold when it is overwhelmed. Immunocompromised people lose this protection. This relates to the emerging hallmark of avoiding immune destruction, targeted by immune-activating anti-CTLA4 monoclonal antibodies.","w":["Cellular energetics relates to metabolism.","Angiogenesis relates to blood supply.","Immortality relates to unlimited division.","Proliferative signalling relates to growth signals."],"k":"Immunocompromise removes immune protection against cancer.","rn":[["","How do cancer cells avoid the immune system?","How do cancer cells avoid the immune system?","Change expressed surface molecules"]]},{"id":48081,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"Which emerging hallmark reflects cancer cells' need for energy to grow?","a":["deregulating cellular energetics","cellular energetics","deregulated cellular energetics"],"e":"Deregulating cellular energetics is an emerging hallmark: cells need energy to grow, and metabolism is very important. Aerobic glycolysis inhibitors target it, interfering with glycolysis and blocking the energy cancer cells need.","k":"Cellular energetics: aerobic glycolysis inhibitors.","rn":[["","Why is deregulating cellular energetics a hallmark?","Why is deregulating cellular energetics a hallmark?","Cells need energy to grow; Metabolism"]]},{"id":48082,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"According to Lazebnik (2010), which original hallmark seems to be the only one that distinguishes malignant from benign tumours?","o":["Activating invasion and metastasis","Sustaining proliferative signalling","Resisting cell death","Inducing angiogenesis","Enabling replicative immortality"],"e":"Lazebnik (2010) argued that some hallmarks relate to benign tumours as well as malignant ones, and that the words tumour and cancer seem to be used interchangeably. Invasion and metastasis seems to be the only original hallmark that distinguishes between malignant and benign tumours.","w":["Benign tumours also proliferate.","This is not singled out as distinguishing.","This is not singled out as distinguishing.","This is not singled out as distinguishing."],"k":"Only invasion and metastasis separate malignant from benign.","rn":[["","Which original hallmark alone distinguishes malignant from benign?","Which original hallmark alone distinguishes malignant from benign?","Invasion and metastasis"]]},{"id":48083,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"Which statement best summarises the critique of the hallmarks of cancer?","o":["Not everyone agrees, and criticism plus new data led to updates","The hallmarks were withdrawn completely after the 2010 critique was published","All authors agree the original hallmarks are complete","The critique showed all hallmarks apply only to benign tumours","The hallmarks have never been updated since 2000"],"e":"Not everyone is in complete agreement about the hallmarks. Lazebnik (2010) argued some apply to benign tumours too; Sonnenschein and Soto (2013) were quite critical; Fouad and Aanei (2017) suggested an update. The authors listened, and criticism plus new data are why the hallmarks keep being updated (2011, 2022).","w":["The original six still stand.","Not everyone agrees.","Lazebnik said some apply to benign tumours as well as malignant.","They were updated in 2011 and 2022."],"k":"Hallmarks are debated and updated over time.","rn":[["","Why have the hallmarks been updated?","Why have the hallmarks been updated?","More research and data"]]},{"id":48084,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"Which drug class is matched to the hallmark of sustaining proliferative signalling?","o":["EGFR (epidermal growth factor receptor) inhibitors","Telomerase inhibitors (blocking chromosome cap maintenance)","PARP (poly ADP-ribose polymerase) inhibitors","Proapoptotic BH3 mimetics","Aerobic glycolysis inhibitors"],"e":"On the drug-targets figure, EGFR inhibitors target sustaining proliferative signalling. Telomerase inhibitors target replicative immortality, PARP inhibitors target genome instability and mutation, proapoptotic BH3 mimetics target resisting cell death, and aerobic glycolysis inhibitors target deregulated cellular energetics.","w":["Telomerase inhibitors target replicative immortality.","PARP inhibitors target genome instability.","BH3 mimetics target resisting cell death.","Glycolysis inhibitors target cellular energetics."],"k":"Proliferative signalling ← EGFR inhibitors.","rn":[["","What does EGFR stand for?","What does EGFR stand for?","Epidermal growth factor receptor"],["","Proto-oncogene c-erbB produces which protein?","Proto-oncogene c-erbB produces which protein?","EGFR (epidermal growth factor receptor)"]]},{"id":48085,"t":"Targeting the Hallmarks","s":"Which drug class targets the enabling characteristic of genome instability and mutation?","a":["parp inhibitors","parp inhibitor","poly adp-ribose polymerase inhibitors","poly (adp-ribose) polymerase inhibitors"],"e":"On the drug-targets figure, PARP (poly (ADP-ribose) polymerase) inhibitors are matched to genome instability and mutation. Other pairings include EGFR inhibitors for proliferative signalling and inhibitors of VEGF signalling for angiogenesis.","k":"Genome instability ← PARP inhibitors.","rn":[["","Why are angiogenesis inhibitors only given for limited time?","Why are angiogenesis inhibitors only given for limited time?","Wounds must heal"],["","What 2 EGFR sites do EGFR inhibitors target?","What 2 EGFR sites do EGFR inhibitors target?","Ligand binding site (outside); Tyrosine kinase activity (inside)"],["","What do telomerase inhibitors block?","What do telomerase inhibitors block?","Replicative immortality"]]},{"id":48086,"t":"Targeting the Hallmarks","s":"Which drug class is matched to the hallmark of evading growth suppressors?","o":["Cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitors","Inhibitors of HGF/c-Met","Selective anti-inflammatory drugs","Inhibitors of VEGF signalling","Anti-CTLA4 monoclonal antibody"],"e":"On the figure, cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitors target evading growth suppressors. HGF/c-Met inhibitors target invasion and metastasis, selective anti-inflammatory drugs target tumour-promoting inflammation, VEGF signalling inhibitors target angiogenesis and anti-CTLA4 mAb targets avoiding immune destruction.","w":["HGF/c-Met inhibitors target invasion and metastasis.","Anti-inflammatories target tumour-promoting inflammation.","VEGF inhibitors target angiogenesis.","Anti-CTLA4 targets avoiding immune destruction."],"k":"Evading growth suppressors ← CDK inhibitors.","rn":[["","What 2 EGFR sites do EGFR inhibitors target?","What 2 EGFR sites do EGFR inhibitors target?","Ligand binding site (outside); Tyrosine kinase activity (inside)"]]},{"id":48087,"t":"Targeting the Hallmarks","s":"Which drug class is matched to activating invasion and metastasis?","a":["inhibitors of hgf/c-met","hgf/c-met inhibitors","c-met inhibitors","hgf inhibitors","hgf c-met inhibitors"],"e":"On the figure, inhibitors of HGF/c-Met (hepatocyte growth factor and its receptor) target activating invasion and metastasis. Invasion and metastasis is the original hallmark that seems to distinguish malignant from benign tumours.","k":"Invasion/metastasis ← HGF/c-Met inhibitors."},{"id":48088,"t":"Targeting the Hallmarks","s":"Which drug class is matched to the hallmark of resisting cell death?","o":["Proapoptotic BH3 mimetics","Telomerase inhibitors","Aerobic glycolysis inhibitors","Cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitors","EGFR inhibitors"],"e":"Resisting cell death means avoiding apoptosis. On the figure, proapoptotic BH3 (BCL-2 homology domain 3) mimetics target it, pushing cells towards apoptosis. Telomerase inhibitors target immortality and glycolysis inhibitors target cellular energetics.","w":["Telomerase inhibitors target replicative immortality.","Glycolysis inhibitors target cellular energetics.","CDK inhibitors target evading growth suppressors.","EGFR inhibitors target proliferative signalling."],"k":"Resisting cell death ← BH3 mimetics."},{"id":48089,"t":"Targeting the Hallmarks","s":"How do EGFR inhibitors stop cancer cells growing?","o":["They target the ligand binding site or the tyrosine kinase activity, blocking signalling","They block VEGFA from binding VEGFR2 on endothelial cells, so no new vessels can form near the tumour","They stop telomeres degrading in cancer cells","They remove methyl groups from tumour suppressor promoters","They activate T cells by blocking CTLA4"],"e":"EGFR inhibitors target either the outside of the receptor (the ligand binding site) or the inside (the tyrosine kinase activity). This blocks signalling, so cancer cells stop growing. EGFR is overexpressed in a number of cancers, making it a good drug target.","w":["This describes VEGF signalling inhibitors.","Telomerase inhibitors block immortality.","This is not described for EGFR inhibitors.","This describes anti-CTLA4 mAb."],"k":"EGFR inhibitors block the ligand site or kinase activity.","rn":[["","What 2 EGFR sites do EGFR inhibitors target?","What 2 EGFR sites do EGFR inhibitors target?","Ligand binding site (outside); Tyrosine kinase activity (inside)"]]},{"id":48090,"t":"Targeting the Hallmarks","s":"A patient on an immune-activating anti-CTLA4 monoclonal antibody develops severe inflammation of normal organs. What best explains this?","o":["The immunotherapy works too well and attacks normal cells","The drug blocks VEGF and stops normal wound healing","The drug inhibits telomerase in normal cells","The drug causes new mutations in normal cells","The drug blocks oestrogen receptors in normal tissue"],"e":"Anti-CTLA4 mAb is an antibody-based targeted therapy called immunotherapy. Cancer cells evade the immune system by changing the molecules on their surface (due to mutations), and immunotherapy reverses that. It has been a miracle for some patients, but others had really bad side effects, some nearly fatal, often when it works too well and attacks normal cells, so patients must be monitored carefully.","w":["This describes VEGF inhibitor effects.","Telomerase inhibition is a separate drug class.","This is not described.","Oestrogen receptor blockade is tamoxifen."],"k":"Immunotherapy side effects: over-activity attacking normal cells.","rn":[["","Why can immunotherapy harm patients?","Why can immunotherapy harm patients?","Works too well; Attacks normal cells"]]},{"id":48091,"t":"Targeting the Hallmarks","s":"How do telomerase inhibitors target enabling replicative immortality?","o":["They block the mechanism that stops chromosome end caps degrading in cancer cells","They prevent ligand binding to the EGFR extracellular domain","They inhibit glycolysis to cut off cancer cell energy","They block the BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase in leukaemia","They activate apoptosis by mimicking the BH3 domain"],"e":"Chromosomes have caps on their ends that normally degrade over time; in cancer this does not happen. Telomerase inhibitors block this immortality. In the Myc pathway, telomerase leads to immortalization.","w":["This describes EGFR inhibitors.","This describes aerobic glycolysis inhibitors.","BCR-ABL is a separate target.","This describes BH3 mimetics."],"k":"Telomerase inhibitors block immortality.","rn":[["","What does blocking EGFR signalling do?","What does blocking EGFR signalling do?","Stops cancer cells growing"],["","What happens to chromosome caps in cancer?","What happens to chromosome caps in cancer?","They do not degrade"]]},{"id":48092,"t":"Targeting the Hallmarks","s":"Which drug class is matched to the emerging hallmark of deregulating cellular energetics?","o":["Aerobic glycolysis inhibitors","Selective anti-inflammatory drugs","PARP inhibitors","Inhibitors of VEGF signalling","Telomerase inhibitors"],"e":"Aerobic glycolysis inhibitors interfere with glycolysis, blocking the energy cancer cells need. They target deregulating cellular energetics. Selective anti-inflammatory drugs target tumour-promoting inflammation.","w":["Anti-inflammatories target tumour-promoting inflammation.","PARP inhibitors target genome instability.","VEGF inhibitors target angiogenesis.","Telomerase inhibitors target replicative immortality."],"k":"Cellular energetics ← aerobic glycolysis inhibitors.","rn":[["","What do aerobic glycolysis inhibitors interfere with?","What do aerobic glycolysis inhibitors interfere with?","Glycolysis (cancer energy)"]]},{"id":48093,"t":"Targeting the Hallmarks","s":"Which drug class is matched to tumour-promoting inflammation?","a":["selective anti-inflammatory drugs","anti-inflammatory drugs","anti-inflammatories","selective anti-inflammatories"],"e":"On the figure, selective anti-inflammatory drugs target tumour-promoting inflammation, an enabling characteristic. They stop inflammation being produced. An example of tumour-promoting inflammation is particulates irritating the lungs and recruiting cytokines.","k":"Tumour-promoting inflammation ← anti-inflammatories.","rn":[["","Which drugs counter tumour-promoting inflammation?","Which drugs counter tumour-promoting inflammation?","Anti-inflammatory drugs"]]},{"id":48094,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"Why is the hallmarks framework useful for drug development?","o":["Each hallmark is a target, and new drugs are designed with a hallmark in mind","It shows that one drug can treat every cancer equally, whatever hallmarks the tumour shows","It proves that surgery is never needed for cancer","It replaces the need for tumour grading and staging","It shows that only inherited cancers can be treated"],"e":"Understanding the hallmarks means we can target them with drugs. Any new drug is designed with a hallmark in mind, and the figure matches a drug class to each hallmark and characteristic, e.g. EGFR inhibitors, VEGF inhibitors, anti-CTLA4, telomerase inhibitors and PARP inhibitors.","w":["Different drug classes target different hallmarks.","Surgery remains a key treatment.","Hallmarks do not replace grading and staging.","Acquired cancers are targeted too."],"k":"Hallmarks guide drug design.","rn":[["","Why is EGFR a good drug target?","Why is EGFR a good drug target?","Overexpressed in many cancers"],["","Why does understanding the hallmarks matter for treatment?","Why does understanding the hallmarks matter for treatment?","We can target them"]]},{"id":48095,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"Why is EGFR described as a good drug target?","o":["It is overexpressed in a number of cancers","It is absent from all cancer cells","It is only found in viruses","It is a tumour suppressor lost in cancer","It is never mutated in human tumours"],"e":"Other acquired gene mutations include overexpression of EGFR and HER2. EGFR is overexpressed in a number of cancers, so it is a good drug target; HER2 is also targeted (by the drug Herceptin). EGFR inhibitors target sustaining proliferative signalling.","w":["It is overexpressed in cancers.","c-erbB is a normal cellular gene; v-erbB is the viral form.","EGFR is a growth receptor made by a proto-oncogene.","Truncated EGFR (v-erbB) is an oncogene."],"k":"EGFR overexpression makes it a good target.","rn":[["","Why is EGFR a good drug target?","Why is EGFR a good drug target?","Overexpressed in many cancers"]]},{"id":48096,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"Which drug is named as targeting HER2 (human epidermal growth factor receptor 2)?","a":["herceptin","trastuzumab"],"e":"Other acquired gene mutations on the slide include overexpression of EGFR and HER2. HER2 is targeted by the drug Herceptin. Many treatments now target specific molecules that are overexpressed or mutated in cancers.","k":"HER2 ← Herceptin."},{"id":48097,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"Which factor is listed as causing cancer through DNA mutations and many carcinogens, with a very high lung cancer risk?","o":["Smoking","Reproductive life","Hormones","Inherited genes","Translocations"],"e":"Smoking causes DNA mutations, with many carcinogens (cancer-causing chemicals) in cigarette smoke. It is very addictive, and GPs spend a lot of time helping people stop because lung cancer risk is very high. People smoking in the 1950s did not know.","w":["Reproductive life includes not having children and promiscuity.","Hormones include high oestrogen.","Inherited genes include BRCA1/2.","Translocations include the Philadelphia chromosome."],"k":"Smoking: carcinogens → DNA mutations → lung cancer.","rn":[["","How does smoking cause cancer?","How does smoking cause cancer?","DNA mutations; Carcinogens in cigarette smoke"]]},{"id":48098,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"Which example is given under \"reproductive life\" as a factor that can cause cancer?","o":["Not having children","High oestrogen levels","HPV infection","BRCA1 mutation","Tamoxifen use"],"e":"Reproductive life factors on the slide are not having children and sexual promiscuity. High oestrogen and tamoxifen are listed under hormones, HPV under viruses and BRCA1 under inherited genes.","w":["This is under hormones.","This is under viruses.","This is under inherited genes.","This is under hormones."],"k":"Reproductive life: no children, promiscuity.","rn":[["","Which 2 reproductive life factors raise cancer risk?","Which 2 reproductive life factors raise cancer risk?","Not having children; Sexual promiscuity"]]},{"id":48099,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"A man asks whether cancer is caused by \"nature or nurture\". Which answer reflects the lecture?","o":["Both: inherited genes and natural processes, plus external factors","Only nature, because cancer is purely an inherited disease passed down within families","Only nurture, because only external factors damage DNA","Neither, because cancer is caused only by viruses","Only nature, because only translocations cause cancer"],"e":"Cancer arises from both. Nature includes inherited genes (BRCA1/2) and natural cell processes (\"we are mutating as we sit here\"). Nurture includes smoking, diet, reproductive life, viruses, pollutants, hormones and radiation.","w":["External factors also play a role.","Inherited genes and natural processes also play a role.","Viruses are one factor among many.","Translocations are one factor among many."],"k":"Nature and nurture both cause cancer.","rn":[["","Why are natural cell processes a cause of cancer?","Why are natural cell processes a cause of cancer?","We constantly mutate"],["","What are the inherited gene examples of cancer risk?","What are the inherited gene examples of cancer risk?","BRCA1; BRCA2"]]},{"id":48100,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"Which type of radiation-related factor is shown causing DNA damage, as demonstrated in the transformation assay?","a":["ionising radiation","ionizing radiation","x-rays","x-ray","radiation"],"e":"Ionising radiation and sunlight are listed as causing DNA damage. In the transformation assay, X-ray treatment of hamster embryo cells produced transformed cells that lost contact inhibition and piled up.","k":"Ionising radiation damages DNA.","rn":[["","How do ionising radiation and sunlight cause cancer?","How do ionising radiation and sunlight cause cancer?","DNA damage"]]},{"id":48101,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"A DNA repair protein gene has one normal and one mutated allele. The protein works, but less well than it should, and some abnormal growth results. Which statement is correct?","o":["This is haploinsufficiency of a tumour suppressor","This is a dominant oncogene with gain of function","This is a recessive tumour suppressor with both alleles lost","This is an epigenetic change in gene expression","This shows the protein is fully switched off"],"e":"In the figure, a tumour suppressor (e.g. a DNA repair protein) with one mutated allele can be haploinsufficient: a lower gene dose leads to abnormal growth. The protein still works a little but not as well, because it works at a lower dose; it is not switched off completely. \"There's always an exception to the rule.\"","w":["DNA repair proteins are tumour suppressors, and this is a loss, not gain.","Only one allele is mutated.","This is a mutation, not an epigenetic change.","The protein still works a little."],"k":"One DNA repair allele lost → lower dose → haploinsufficiency.","rn":[["","Oncogenes vs tumour suppressor genes: number known","Oncogenes vs tumour suppressor genes: number known","More than 100 oncogenes; At least 15 TSGs"],["","What are cancer associated genes?","What are cancer associated genes?","Oncogenes; Tumour Suppressor Genes"],["","What are the 2 major classifications of mutated genes in cancer?","What are the 2 major classifications of mutated genes in cancer?","Oncogenes; Tumour suppressor genes"]]},{"id":48102,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"How many tumour suppressor genes are stated to be known?","o":["At least 15","More than 100","Exactly 6","About 200","Fewer than 5"],"e":"More than 100 oncogenes are known, and at least 15 tumour suppressor genes. There are many more oncogenes because only one allele needs to be mutated to activate one, which is far more likely than both alleles of a tumour suppressor being hit.","w":["More than 100 is the figure for oncogenes.","Six is the number of original hallmarks.","About 200 is the number of cancer types.","At least 15 are known."],"k":"≥15 tumour suppressors; >100 oncogenes.","rn":[["","Oncogenes vs tumour suppressor genes: number known","Oncogenes vs tumour suppressor genes: number known","More than 100 oncogenes; At least 15 TSGs"]]},{"id":48103,"t":"Molecular Basis of Cancer","s":"A tumour suppressor gene that is a growth inhibitor has both alleles mutated. What is the result?","o":["Loss of the growth inhibitor, giving abnormal growth","Gain of a growth inducer, giving abnormal growth","Lower gene dose but normal growth","Increased contact inhibition","Increased apoptosis of the mutated cells"],"e":"In the figure, a tumour suppressor gene that is a growth inhibitor needs both alleles mutated (recessive). This causes loss of the growth inhibitor and abnormal growth. An oncogene growth inducer needs only one mutated allele (dominant).","w":["Gain of a growth inducer is an oncogene.","Losing both alleles causes abnormal growth.","Loss of growth control reduces contact inhibition.","Damaged cells avoid apoptosis."],"k":"Both inhibitor alleles lost → abnormal growth.","rn":[["","What kind of cell growth do DNA changes cause?","What kind of cell growth do DNA changes cause?","Abnormal (neoplastic) growth"],["","What is haploinsufficiency?","What is haploinsufficiency?","One TSG allele mutated; Lower gene dose; Still abnormal growth"]]},{"id":48104,"t":"Cell Cycle and Cancer","s":"What is the name of the point in G1 where tumour suppressors give the growth inhibitory signal, marked \"R\" on the figure?","a":["restriction point","the restriction point","r point"],"e":"In the cell-cycle figure, tumour suppressors (p53 and pRb) give the growth inhibitory signal at R, the restriction point in G1. Cells with lots of mutations normally go into apoptosis from here. If these genes are mutated, the cell carries on through the cycle.","k":"R = restriction point in G1.","rn":[["","Where do P53 and pRb act in the cell cycle?","Where do P53 and pRb act in the cell cycle?","Restriction point (R); In G1"]]},{"id":48105,"t":"Molecular Basis of Cancer","s":"A cell has lots of DNA damage at the restriction point. With working p53 and pRb, what normally happens?","o":["The cell receives a growth inhibitory signal and may go into apoptosis","The cell speeds through S phase to repair the damage","The cell becomes immortalised and keeps dividing","The cell loses contact inhibition and piles up","The cell activates Myc to drive proliferation"],"e":"Tumour suppressors p53 and pRb give a growth inhibitory signal at the restriction point, and cells with lots of mutations go into apoptosis. If they are mutated, the cell carries on through the cycle and keeps growing. p53 is the guardian of the genome.","w":["The tumour suppressors stop the cycle.","Immortalisation follows failure of these genes.","Loss of contact inhibition is a transformed feature.","Myc gives a proliferation signal, which the tumour suppressors oppose."],"k":"Working p53/pRb → stop or apoptosis.","rn":[["","What happens if p53 is mutated?","What happens if p53 is mutated?","Cell cycle carries on; Damaged cells avoid apoptosis"],["","What should tumour suppressors do to heavily mutated cells?","What should tumour suppressors do to heavily mutated cells?","Inhibit growth; Send to apoptosis"]]},{"id":48106,"t":"Cell Cycle and Cancer","s":"In the cell-cycle wheel, which phase is described as growth and preparation for mitosis?","a":["g2","g2 phase","second growth phase"],"e":"The wheel shows G1 (first growth phase: growth and normal metabolic roles), S (synthesis phase: DNA replication), G2 (second growth phase: growth and preparation for mitosis) and the mitotic phase (prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase).","k":"G2 = growth and preparation for mitosis."},{"id":48107,"t":"Examples of Cancer Associated Genes","s":"Which genes are shown as the cancer-associated genes mutated late in the multistep model?","o":["ras, Rb, p53 and p16","BCR, ABL, Myc and VEGF","EGFR, HER2, ER and PDGF","BRCA1, BRCA2, HPV and CTLA4","Telomerase, PARP, VEGFR2 and c-Met"],"e":"In the multistep figure, after the mutator phenotype, mutations hit cancer-associated genes: ras, Rb, p53 and p16 (oncogenes and tumour suppressor genes). These lead to cancer progression, invasion and metastasis.","w":["These are not the genes shown in the multistep figure.","These are receptors and growth factors, not the figure's genes.","HPV is a virus and CTLA4 an immune molecule.","These are drug targets."],"k":"Multistep figure genes: ras, Rb, p53, p16.","rn":[["","Examples of Cancer Associated Genes","Examples of Cancer Associated Genes","ras; Rb; p53; p16"]]},{"id":48108,"t":"Aetiology of Cancer","s":"Which three sources of mutation are shown hitting cells at the start of the multistep model?","o":["X-rays, chemicals and endogenous processes","Viruses, hormones and translocations","Inherited genes, BRCA1 and BRCA2","Diet, exercise and reproductive life","Angiogenesis, inflammation and immunity"],"e":"The multistep figure shows X-rays, chemicals and endogenous processes (the body's own processes) hitting cells, with one cell gaining an early mutation in a genome-stability gene. Mutations happen all the time from these sources, and DNA repair fixes most.","w":["These are other risk factors, not the figure's sources.","These are inherited genes.","These are lifestyle risk factors.","These are hallmarks or characteristics."],"k":"Mutation sources: X-rays, chemicals, endogenous processes.","rn":[["","What can cause Carcinogenesis?","What can cause Carcinogenesis?","X-rays; Chemicals; Endogenous Processes"]]},{"id":48109,"t":"Molecular Basis of Cancer","s":"How has the role of molecular biology in cancer diagnosis changed?","o":["Diagnosis once relied on tissues; molecular biology is now also used, including in screening","Molecular biology has completely replaced tissue diagnosis","Molecular biology is used only in research, never in hospitals","Diagnosis now relies only on imaging such as X-ray and scanning, with no tissue or molecular tests needed at all","Molecular biology is used only after a patient has died"],"e":"In the past, cancers were diagnosed by looking at tissues; now molecular biology is also used, and it is an important part of screening for cancers in hospitals. Tissue diagnosis still has its place.","w":["It is used alongside tissue diagnosis.","It is used in hospital screening.","Imaging is not the only approach.","It is used in patient care."],"k":"Diagnosis now combines tissue and molecular biology.","rn":[["","Cancer was once diagnosed from tissues; what is now also used?","Cancer was once diagnosed from tissues; what is now also used?","Molecular biology"]]},{"id":48110,"t":"Molecular Basis of Cancer","s":"In the Myc figure, which pathway leads from Myc to apoptosis?","o":["p19ARF → p53 → apoptosis","Telomerase → p53 → apoptosis","Cdc25A → CyclinE-Cdk2 → apoptosis","p27 → telomerase → apoptosis","Ras-Raf → telomerase → apoptosis"],"e":"In the figure, Myc outputs to telomerase (immortalization), to p27 (blocked) and Cdc25A leading to CyclinE-Cdk2 (proliferation), and to p19ARF leading to p53 and apoptosis. Ras-Raf feeds into Myc.","w":["Telomerase leads to immortalization.","CyclinE-Cdk2 leads to proliferation.","p27 is on the proliferation route, and telomerase leads to immortalization.","Ras-Raf feeds into Myc, upstream."],"k":"Myc → p19ARF → p53 → apoptosis.","rn":[["","What happens if p53 is mutated?","What happens if p53 is mutated?","Cell cycle carries on; Damaged cells avoid apoptosis"]]},{"id":48111,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"Which inputs are shown at the top of the Myc signalling figure?","o":["Growth stimulatory, DNA damage, growth inhibitory and death receptor signals","Oestrogen, progesterone, testosterone and cortisol","VEGFA, VEGFR2, HGF and c-Met","Smoking, diet, viruses and pollutants","Histone acetylation, histone methylation and DNA methylation"],"e":"The top inputs of the Myc figure are growth stimulatory signals, DNA damage, growth inhibitory signals and death receptor signals. Ras-Raf feeds into Myc, which outputs to telomerase, the cell-cycle machinery and p53.","w":["Hormones are not the figure's inputs.","These are angiogenesis and invasion molecules.","These are risk factors.","These are epigenetic mechanisms."],"k":"Myc integrates growth, damage, inhibitory and death signals.","rn":[["","How do ionising radiation and sunlight cause cancer?","How do ionising radiation and sunlight cause cancer?","DNA damage"],["","What does EGFR stand for?","What does EGFR stand for?","Epidermal growth factor receptor"],["","What kind of cell growth do DNA changes cause?","What kind of cell growth do DNA changes cause?","Abnormal (neoplastic) growth"]]},{"id":48112,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"In which leukaemia is the Philadelphia chromosome observed in most patients?","a":["chronic myelogenous leukaemia","cml","chronic myeloid leukaemia","chronic myelogenous leukemia","chronic myeloid leukemia"],"e":"The Philadelphia chromosome is observed in most patients with chronic myelogenous leukaemia (CML). It is a translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22 producing a BCR-ABL fusion gene with high tyrosine kinase activity. It is another good way of targeting treatments.","k":"Philadelphia chromosome → CML.","rn":[["","Philadelphia chromosome is observed in most patients with what?","Philadelphia chromosome is observed in most patients with what?","CML (chronic myelogenous leukaemia)"],["","What does CML stand for?","What does CML stand for?","Chronic myelogenous leukaemia"],["","Image card: What is the abnormal chromosome on the right called? Which fusion gene and disease?","What is the abnormal chromosome on the right called? Which fusion gene and disease?","Philadelphia chromosome (22q-); BCR-ABL fusion; CML (chronic myelogenous leukaemia)"]]},{"id":48113,"t":"Assays of Oncogenic Transformation","s":"After how many days are colonies scored in the hamster embryo transformation assay?","o":["8–10 days","1–2 days","3–4 weeks","24 hours","6 months"],"e":"In the transformation assay, single cells are seeded, treated with radiation or chemicals, and colonies are scored after 8–10 days after staining. Transformed clones are identified morphologically.","w":["This is too short.","This is too long.","This is too short.","This is far too long."],"k":"Colonies scored at 8–10 days.","rn":[["","When are colonies scored in the transformation assay?","When are colonies scored in the transformation assay?","8-10 days; After staining"]]},{"id":48114,"t":"Assays of Oncogenic Transformation","s":"In the transformation assay, how are transformed clones identified?","o":["Morphologically, by their shape","By sequencing each colony's genome","By injecting each colony into a mouse","By measuring oestrogen receptor levels","By painting their chromosomes"],"e":"The assay figure labels \"transformed clones identified morphologically\", meaning by their shape. Transformed cells are densely stained, piled up and randomly arranged, unlike orderly, contact-inhibited normal cells.","w":["Sequencing is not used in the assay.","Mouse injection belongs to the DNA transfection experiment.","ER levels are not part of the assay.","Chromosome painting is for karyotyping."],"k":"Transformed clones are identified by morphology."},{"id":48115,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"On the epigenetics slide, which health endpoints are listed?","o":["Cancer, autoimmune disease, mental disorders and diabetes","Cancer, fractures, cataracts and anaemia","Infection, burns, fractures and asthma","Cancer only, with no other endpoints","Leukaemia, lymphoma, sarcoma and carcinoma"],"e":"The epigenetics slide shows health endpoints of cancer, autoimmune disease, mental disorders and diabetes. Epigenetic mechanisms are affected by development, environmental chemicals, drugs, ageing and diet.","w":["These are not the endpoints shown.","These are not the endpoints shown.","Other endpoints are shown besides cancer.","These are cancer types."],"k":"Epigenetic endpoints: cancer, autoimmune, mental, diabetes.","rn":[["","What are the 4 health endpoints of epigenetic changes?","What are the 4 health endpoints of epigenetic changes?","Cancer; Autoimmune disease; Mental disorders; Diabetes"]]},{"id":48116,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"A tumour's cells avoid every molecule that would normally stop them growing. Which hallmark is this?","a":["evading growth suppressors","evading growth suppression"],"e":"Evading growth suppressors means avoiding any molecules that stop the cancer from growing. It is targeted by cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitors. It is one of the six original hallmarks.","k":"Avoiding stop signals = evading growth suppressors.","rn":[["","Evading growth suppressors means evading what?","Evading growth suppressors means evading what?","Molecules that stop growth"]]},{"id":48117,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"Why does the immune system not always prevent cancer?","o":["It can kill a few cancer cells, but cancer takes hold once it is overwhelmed","It cannot recognise any cancer cell under any circumstances","It only acts on benign tumours","It is switched off by VEGFA in all patients","It destroys only normal cells, never cancer cells"],"e":"The immune system can kill a few cancer cells, and cancer takes hold when it is overwhelmed. Cancer cells evade the immune system by changing the molecules on their surface because of mutations. Immunocompromised people lose this protection.","w":["It can kill a few cancer cells.","This is not stated.","VEGFA drives angiogenesis.","It can kill cancer cells."],"k":"Immune protection works until it is overwhelmed.","rn":[["","When does the immune system fail to kill cancer cells?","When does the immune system fail to kill cancer cells?","When overwhelmed"]]},{"id":48118,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"In which year were the hallmarks updated in \"Hallmarks of Cancer: New Dimensions\" by Hanahan?","o":["2022","2000","2011","2010","2017"],"e":"The hallmarks were first described in 2000 and updated in 2011 (emerging hallmarks and enabling characteristics). In 2022 Hanahan published \"Hallmarks of Cancer: New Dimensions\". Lazebnik's critique was 2010 and Fouad and Aanei's suggested update was 2017.","w":["2000 was the first description.","2011 was the next-generation update.","2010 was Lazebnik's critique.","2017 was Fouad and Aanei's suggestion."],"k":"Hallmarks: 2000, 2011, 2022.","rn":[["","In which later years were the hallmarks developed further?","In which later years were the hallmarks developed further?","2022; Another this year"]]},{"id":48119,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"In which normal situations is angiogenesis important?","o":["In the embryo and in wound healing","Only in tumours, never normally","In adults continuously in all tissues","Only during infection","Only in bone growth"],"e":"Angiogenesis is important in the embryo so the circulation can develop, and in wound healing; adults should have little going on otherwise. Cancer cells take over this process.","w":["It is also a normal process.","Adults should have little going on.","Infection is not the example given.","Bone growth is not the example given."],"k":"Normal angiogenesis: embryo and wound healing.","rn":[["","When should angiogenesis normally happen?","When should angiogenesis normally happen?","Wound healing; Embryonic development"],["","Why are angiogenesis inhibitors only given for limited time?","Why are angiogenesis inhibitors only given for limited time?","Wounds must heal"],["","What is an everyday example of contact inhibition?","What is an everyday example of contact inhibition?","Wound healing"]]},{"id":48120,"t":"Cell Cycle and Cancer","s":"Which statement about the role of proto-oncogenes Ras and Myc in normal cells is correct?","o":["They give a proliferation signal only when needed","They permanently block all cell division","They protect DNA integrity during S phase","They give the growth inhibitory signal at R","They trigger apoptosis of all damaged cells"],"e":"In normal cells, Ras and Myc (as proto-oncogenes) give a proliferation signal in G1 only when needed. If mutated, cells proliferate uncontrollably. p53 and pRb give the growth inhibitory signal at the restriction point, and BRCA1/2 protect DNA integrity in S.","w":["They promote rather than block division.","This is the role of genomic stability genes.","This is the role of p53 and pRb.","Apoptosis is linked to the tumour suppressors."],"k":"Normal Ras/Myc: proliferation signal when needed.","rn":[["","Proto-oncogene vs oncogene: proliferation","Proto-oncogene vs oncogene: proliferation","Proto-oncogene: only when needed; Oncogene: uncontrollably"]]},{"id":48121,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"A 52-year-old man has fatigue and a very high white cell count. A bone marrow karyotype with chromosome painting shows that part of chromosome 9 now sits on chromosome 22, and part of 22 sits on 9. Chromosome 22 is visibly shorter than normal. Molecular testing finds a fused gene.<br><br>What is the fused gene, and how does it drive his disease?","o":["BCR-ABL; very high tyrosine kinase activity drives uncontrolled proliferation","BCR-ABL; loss of tyrosine kinase activity prevents the leukaemic cells entering apoptosis","v-erbB; a receptor missing its extracellular domain signals constantly","v-sis; truncated PDGF activates the pathway at the wrong times","TP53; loss of the guardian of the genome lets damaged cells survive"],"e":"Step 1: a swap between chromosomes 9 and 22, giving a shortened 22q− (the Philadelphia chromosome), joins BCR and ABL into a BCR-ABL fusion gene, seen in most patients with chronic myelogenous leukaemia (CML). Step 2: the fusion has a very high amount of tyrosine kinase activity, sending signals to the nucleus, so cells divide and proliferate uncontrollably. This gain of function makes BCR-ABL an oncogene and a good treatment target.","w":["BCR-ABL activity is increased, not lost.","v-erbB is a truncated EGFR, not a translocation.","v-sis produces truncated PDGF.","The finding is a translocation fusion gene, not a TP53 mutation."],"k":"t(9;22) → BCR-ABL → high tyrosine kinase → CML.","rn":[["","What activity is very high in the BCR-ABL fusion?","What activity is very high in the BCR-ABL fusion?","Tyrosine kinase activity"],["","What does BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase signalling cause?","What does BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase signalling cause?","Uncontrolled division and proliferation"]]},{"id":48122,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"A research team studies two families. In family A, cancer develops in people who inherit one mutated copy of a gene, and cells with just that one change grow without regulation. In family B, tumours only form after a second, acquired mutation knocks out the remaining normal copy of a growth-inhibiting gene.<br><br>Which genes are most likely involved in each family?","o":["A: an oncogene acting dominantly; B: a recessive tumour suppressor","A: a recessive tumour suppressor; B: a dominant oncogene","A: a haploinsufficient oncogene; B: an epigenetic modifier","A and B: both dominant oncogenes with gain of function","A and B: both recessive tumour suppressors with loss of function"],"e":"Step 1: in family A one mutated allele is enough to cause growth without regulation; this is the dominant, gain-of-function pattern of an oncogene. Step 2: in family B a growth inhibitor needs both alleles mutated before abnormal growth occurs; this is the recessive, loss-of-function pattern of a tumour suppressor. The exception is haploinsufficiency, where one tumour suppressor allele (e.g. a DNA repair protein) gives a lower dose.","w":["This reverses the two patterns.","Haploinsufficiency applies to tumour suppressors.","Family B needs two hits, which is the tumour suppressor pattern.","Family A needs only one hit, which is the oncogene pattern."],"k":"One hit = oncogene; two hits = tumour suppressor.","rn":[["","Oncogenes vs tumour suppressor genes: effect of mutation","Oncogenes vs tumour suppressor genes: effect of mutation","Oncogene: gain of function; TSG: loss of function"],["","Why are tumour suppressor genes mainly recessive?","Why are tumour suppressor genes mainly recessive?","Both alleles must be mutated"],["","Tumour suppressor genes are mainly dominant or recessive?","Tumour suppressor genes are mainly dominant or recessive?","Recessive"]]},{"id":48123,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"Researchers compare normal fibroblasts with fibroblasts carrying v-erbB. The normal cells signal for growth only when epidermal growth factor is added to the medium. The v-erbB cells signal for growth continuously, even in medium with no growth factor. Antibodies that bind the outside of the receptor block signalling in normal cells but not in the v-erbB cells.<br><br>What best explains these results?","o":["v-erbB encodes EGFR lacking its extracellular ligand-binding domain","v-erbB encodes extra PDGF secreted in the wound response","v-erbB silences EGFR by promoter methylation","v-erbB fuses the EGFR gene to ABL through a translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22","v-erbB makes cells lose all of their EGFR receptors"],"e":"Step 1: c-erbB encodes the EGFR, which normally signals when a ligand binds its extracellular domain. Step 2: v-erbB is a truncated EGFR with the extracellular domain deleted, so it needs no stimulus and works on its own, all the time. With no outer domain, antibodies to the outside of the receptor have nothing to bind.","w":["PDGF is made by c-sis/v-sis.","Methylation would silence the gene, not make it constantly active.","BCR-ABL is the 9;22 fusion.","The receptor is present and constantly active."],"k":"v-erbB = ligand-independent EGFR.","rn":[["","What normally binds the EGFR extracellular domain?","What normally binds the EGFR extracellular domain?","A ligand"],["","Why does v-erbB EGFR signal without a stimulus?","Why does v-erbB EGFR signal without a stimulus?","No extracellular domain; Works on its own"]]},{"id":48124,"t":"Assays of Oncogenic Transformation","s":"A lab tests whether a new industrial chemical can cause cancer. Hamster embryo cells are separated with trypsin and seeded as single cells into two sets of dishes; one set is treated with the chemical and the other left untreated. After 9 days the colonies are fixed with methanol and stained with Giemsa. The treated dishes contain dense, piled-up, randomly arranged colonies, while the control dishes show flat, orderly colonies.<br><br>What do the results show, and what property has been lost?","o":["The chemical causes oncogenic transformation; contact inhibition is lost","The chemical is safe; the piling up is normal wound healing","The chemical causes apoptosis; cell growth has stopped","The chemical causes epigenetic silencing; DNA sequence is lost","The chemical causes angiogenesis; blood supply has been lost"],"e":"Step 1: this is the primary culture assay of oncogenic transformation (dissociate, seed single cells, treat, stain and score colonies after 8–10 days). The same test works for many chemicals. Step 2: the treated cells are densely stained, piled up and randomly arranged, unlike the orderly control cells, so they have lost contact inhibition and been transformed, which resembles a tumour in the body.","w":["Normal cells stop growing on contact; piling up shows transformation.","The treated cells kept growing.","The assay detects transformation by morphology, not epigenetic change.","Angiogenesis cannot occur in a culture dish of these cells."],"k":"Transformed colonies: dense, piled up, random = lost contact inhibition.","rn":[["","Why do transformed cells lose contact inhibition?","Why do transformed cells lose contact inhibition?","Mutations from radiation"]]},{"id":48125,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"A tumour grows rapidly to several centimetres. Samples from its core show cells short of oxygen and nutrients. These cells are found to be secreting a growth factor that binds a receptor on nearby endothelial cells. Imaging of the tumour shows a dense, tangled mesh of vessels.<br><br>Which growth factor and receptor are involved, and which drug class would target this process?","o":["VEGFA and VEGFR2; inhibitors of VEGF signalling","EGF and EGFR; EGFR inhibitors","PDGF and EGFR; telomerase inhibitors","HGF and c-Met; anti-CTLA4 monoclonal antibody","VEGFA and VEGFR2; PARP inhibitors"],"e":"Step 1: cancer cells grow so fast they become hypoxic, and hypoxic cancer cells secrete VEGFA, which activates VEGF receptor 2, leading to new blood vessel growth; tumour vessels are chaotic because they grow quickly and uncontrollably. Step 2: inducing angiogenesis is targeted by inhibitors of VEGF signalling, which are only given for so long because the patient needs to heal properly after any injury.","w":["EGF/EGFR drive proliferative signalling, not angiogenesis.","Telomerase inhibitors target immortality.","HGF/c-Met relates to invasion, and anti-CTLA4 to immune evasion.","PARP inhibitors target genome instability."],"k":"Hypoxia → VEGFA/VEGFR2 → angiogenesis ← VEGF inhibitors.","rn":[["","Which VEGF is secreted by cancer cells?","Which VEGF is secreted by cancer cells?","VEGFA"],["","What does VEGFR2 stand for?","What does VEGFR2 stand for?","VEGF receptor 2"],["","Which receptor does VEGFA activate?","Which receptor does VEGFA activate?","VEGFR2"]]},{"id":48126,"t":"Causes of Cancer","s":"Two women have breast cancer. Tumour 1 has high levels of oestrogen receptor; tumour 2 has none. Both are offered a drug that works through oestrogen receptors. Tumour 1 shrinks on treatment, but tumour 2 continues to grow.<br><br>Which drug is this, and what explains the different responses?","o":["Tamoxifen; it works only in cancers with high ER levels","Herceptin; it works only in cancers with high ER levels","Tamoxifen; it works only in ER-negative cancers","EGFR inhibitor; it works only in ER-negative cancers","PARP inhibitor; it works only in ER-positive cancers"],"e":"Step 1: hormones are a cancer factor, and ER status guides treatment: if a cancer has high oestrogen receptor (ER) levels, certain treatments such as tamoxifen will work. Step 2: tamoxifen will not work in patients without high ER levels, so tumour 2 needs other treatments.","w":["Herceptin targets HER2.","Tamoxifen works in ER-positive cancers.","EGFR inhibitors do not act through ER.","PARP inhibitors target genome instability."],"k":"High ER → tamoxifen works; ER-negative → other treatments.","rn":[["","Which hormone factor raises cancer risk?","Which hormone factor raises cancer risk?","High oestrogen levels"]]},{"id":48127,"t":"Oncogenes and Tumour Suppressor Genes","s":"A 38-year-old woman inherits a mutated BRCA1 gene. Over the years her breast cells accumulate many more mutations than normal, including in ras and p53. Cells that would normally die from this damage survive, and she develops an invasive breast cancer that later spreads to the liver.<br><br>Which description of the process is most accurate?","o":["Genome-stability gene mutation → mutator phenotype → oncogene/TSG mutations → invasion and metastasis","Oncogene activation → contact inhibition → apoptosis → invasion","Epigenetic silencing of BRCA1 → normal DNA repair → benign tumour formation → late metastasis to the liver","Translocation → BCR-ABL → leukaemia → metastasis","Angiogenesis → mutator phenotype → contact inhibition → metastasis"],"e":"Step 1: BRCA1 is a gene that keeps the genome stable; an early mutation in a genome-stability gene overwhelms repair and produces a mutator phenotype, so mutations build up. Step 2: mutations then hit cancer-associated genes (ras, Rb, p53, p16); with p53 lost, damaged cells do not go into apoptosis, leading to cancer progression, invasion and metastasis.","w":["Transformed cells lose contact inhibition and avoid apoptosis.","Repair is overwhelmed, and the cancer is invasive.","BCR-ABL is the CML translocation.","Angiogenesis is not the first step, and contact inhibition is lost."],"k":"BRCA1 loss → mutator phenotype → cancer genes → spread.","rn":[["","Oncogenes vs tumour suppressor genes: effect of mutation","Oncogenes vs tumour suppressor genes: effect of mutation","Oncogene: gain of function; TSG: loss of function"],["","Why does apoptosis fail in cancer cells with many mutations?","Why does apoptosis fail in cancer cells with many mutations?","Lack of genome stability"]]},{"id":48128,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"A patient's lung adenocarcinoma is analysed. Sequencing shows a KRAS mutation. Genome-wide analysis also shows an abnormal DNA methylation pattern at many genes, though their sequences are normal. Some genes in tightly packed chromatin have been switched off.<br><br>What does the methylation finding represent, and when in the cancer's development did it probably occur?","o":["An epigenetic change in gene expression, likely an early event in tumorigenesis","A genetic change in the DNA sequence, likely a late event after metastasis","A translocation between two chromosomes, likely an early event","A gain-of-function oncogene mutation, likely a late event","An epigenetic change that alters the genotype, likely a late event"],"e":"Step 1: altered methylation with normal sequences is an epigenetic change: it alters gene expression (phenotype) but not the DNA sequence (genotype); tightly wound DNA is inaccessible and inactive. Step 2: DNA methylation alterations are early events in tumorigenesis, and specific methylation profiles are seen with EGFR, KRAS and TP53 mutations in lung cancer.","w":["The sequences are normal, and methylation changes are early.","No chromosomal swap is described.","Methylation is not a sequence mutation.","Epigenetic changes do not alter the genotype."],"k":"Methylation changes = epigenetic, early in tumorigenesis.","rn":[["","When do DNA methylation alterations occur in tumorigenesis?","When do DNA methylation alterations occur in tumorigenesis?","Early events"]]},{"id":48129,"t":"Targeting the Hallmarks","s":"A patient's tumour shows overexpression of EGFR, very high telomerase activity, and loss of a key DNA repair pathway. The oncology team plans to choose drugs that target the hallmark or characteristic linked to each finding.<br><br>Which set of drug classes matches these three findings, in order?","o":["EGFR inhibitors, telomerase inhibitors, PARP inhibitors","VEGF inhibitors, BH3 mimetics, anti-inflammatory drugs","EGFR inhibitors, glycolysis inhibitors, CDK inhibitors","HGF/c-Met inhibitors, telomerase inhibitors, anti-CTLA4 mAb","CDK inhibitors, PARP inhibitors, EGFR inhibitors"],"e":"Step 1: EGFR overexpression drives sustaining proliferative signalling; telomerase activity drives enabling replicative immortality; loss of DNA repair reflects genome instability and mutation. Step 2: on the drug-targets figure these are matched with EGFR inhibitors, telomerase inhibitors and PARP inhibitors respectively.","w":["These target angiogenesis, cell death and inflammation.","Glycolysis and CDK inhibitors target energetics and growth suppressors.","HGF/c-Met and anti-CTLA4 target invasion and immune evasion.","This order does not match the findings."],"k":"EGFR → EGFR inhibitors; telomerase → telomerase inhibitors; genome instability → PARP inhibitors.","rn":[["","What do telomerase inhibitors block?","What do telomerase inhibitors block?","Replicative immortality"]]},{"id":48130,"t":"Hallmarks of Cancer","s":"A pathologist reviews a slow-growing tumour that sustains proliferative signalling, resists cell death and has induced some new vessels. It has a well-defined border, and extensive sampling shows no breach of the basement membrane and no spread. A colleague argues it must be malignant because it shows several hallmarks of cancer.<br><br>Which response best reflects the critique of the hallmarks?","o":["Some hallmarks also occur in benign tumours; only invasion and metastasis distinguish malignancy","Any tumour with three hallmarks must be classified as malignant","The hallmarks apply only to leukaemias, not to solid tumours, so they cannot be used for this tumour at all","Angiogenesis alone proves that a tumour is malignant","Hallmarks cannot be seen in any tumour under the microscope"],"e":"Step 1: Lazebnik (2010) argued that some hallmarks relate to benign tumours as well as malignant ones, and that tumour and cancer seem to be used interchangeably. Step 2: invasion and metastasis seems to be the only original hallmark that distinguishes malignant from benign tumours; this tumour has neither, so the other hallmarks alone do not make it malignant.","w":["Several hallmarks can be present in benign tumours.","The hallmarks apply to cancers in general.","Angiogenesis is not the distinguishing hallmark.","The issue is which hallmark distinguishes malignancy."],"k":"Only invasion and metastasis separates malignant from benign.","rn":[["","Which original hallmark alone distinguishes malignant from benign?","Which original hallmark alone distinguishes malignant from benign?","Invasion and metastasis"]]}]);
